OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026", "title": "Rationality of the Canonical Unramified Arthur Filtration", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Rationality-of-the-Canonical-Unramified-Arthur-Filtration-September-24-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "014", "family_title": "Restricted geometric Langlands, global Arthur enhancements, and generic Ramanujan", "abstract_html": "Under the characteristic hypotheses of restricted geometric Langlands theory, we prove that the canonical Arthur filtration on finitely supported unramified automorphic functions is defined over ℚ for split connected semisimple groups. The result includes the noncuspidal part and every closed invariant nilpotent support, establishing the rationality conjecture of Gaitsgory–Lafforgue–Raskin in this setting.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Rationality of the canonical unramified Arthur filtration — original Theorem 1.2, together with the coefficient-prime independence established in Section 7.2.** **Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.** Let …", "statement": "<p>**Rationality of the canonical unramified Arthur filtration — original Theorem 1.2, together with the coefficient-prime independence established in Section 7.2.**</p><p>**Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.**\nLet \\(X/\\mathbb F_q\\) be a smooth projective geometrically connected curve, and let \\(G/\\mathbb F_q\\) be split, connected, and semisimple. Put\n\\[\nk_0=\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q,\\qquad H=G_{k_0},\\qquad \\mathfrak h=\\operatorname{Lie}(H).\n\\]\nImpose the four conditions of original Assumption 1.1:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The Lie algebra \\(\\mathfrak h\\) has an \\(H\\)-invariant nondegenerate symmetric bilinear form whose restriction to the center of \\(\\operatorname{Lie}(M)\\) is nondegenerate for every Levi subgroup \\(M\\subset H\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For every Levi subgroup \\(M\\subset H\\) and maximal torus \\(T_M\\subset M\\), restriction induces an isomorphism\n   \\[\n   k_0[\\mathfrak m]^M\\xrightarrow{\\sim}k_0[\\mathfrak t_M]^{W_M},\n   \\quad\n   \\mathfrak m=\\operatorname{Lie}(M),\\quad\n   \\mathfrak t_M=\\operatorname{Lie}(T_M),\\quad\n   W_M=N_M(T_M)/T_M.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. The scheme-theoretic centralizer in \\(H\\) of every semisimple element of \\(\\mathfrak h\\) is a Levi subgroup.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. For every extension \\(K/k_0\\) and nilpotent \\(n\\in\\mathfrak h\\otimes_{k_0}K\\), there is a parabolic subgroup \\(P\\subset H_K\\), defined over \\(K\\), such that\n   \\[\n   n\\in\\operatorname{Lie}(R_u(P)).\n   \\]</p><p>Here a Levi subgroup means a Levi factor of a parabolic subgroup. Semisimple means geometrically conjugate into the Lie algebra of a maximal torus; nilpotent means that the geometric adjoint orbit closure contains zero.</p><p>Fix a prime \\(\\ell\\ne\\operatorname{char}(\\mathbb F_q)\\), and set \\(E=\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_\\ell\\). Let \\(S\\) be the set of isomorphism classes of \\(G\\)-bundles on \\(X\\) over \\(\\mathbb F_q\\). For a characteristic-zero field \\(L\\), write\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_L=L^{(S)}\n\\]\nfor the finitely supported functions on \\(S\\), with their distinguished point-function basis. Thus\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_E=E\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb Q}.\n\\]</p><p>Fix the pinned split \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-form of the Langlands dual group, and write \\(\\widehat G/E\\) for its scalar extension, \\(\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}\\) for its Lie algebra, and \\(\\widehat{\\mathcal N}\\) for its nilpotent cone. Put\n\\[\n\\overline X=X\\times_{\\mathbb F_q}k_0,\\qquad\n\\mathcal C=\\operatorname{Shv}_{\\operatorname{Nilp}}(\\operatorname{Bun}_G(\\overline X),E),\n\\qquad\n\\Phi=(\\operatorname{Frob}_{\\operatorname{Bun}})_*,\n\\]\nusing geometric Frobenius. The subscript denotes singular support in the global nilpotent cone.</p><p>For a closed \\(\\operatorname{Ad}(\\widehat G)\\)-invariant subset \\(Y\\subset\\widehat{\\mathcal N}\\), let \\(\\mathcal C_Y\\subset\\mathcal C\\) be the derived-Satake support category. Under restricted geometric Langlands,\n\\[\n\\mathcal C\\simeq\\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\widehat{\\mathcal N}}(Z),\n\\qquad\nZ={}'\\operatorname{LocSys}^{\\operatorname{restr}}_{\\widehat G}(\\overline X),\n\\]\nand \\(\\mathcal C_Y\\) corresponds to \\(\\operatorname{IndCoh}_Y(Z)\\). The prime denotes the retained union of connected components occurring in the equivalence. A singular point is a pair \\((\\sigma,A)\\), where \\(A\\) is a horizontal adjoint section; the condition is that \\(A\\) take values in \\(Y\\).</p><p>For a dualizable differential graded category \\(\\mathcal D\\) and endofunctor \\(T\\), let \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(T,\\mathcal D)\\) denote its categorical trace. Using\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi,\\mathcal C)\\simeq\\mathcal A_E,\n\\]\ndefine\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_Y\\mathcal A_E\n=\n\\operatorname{im}\\!\\left[\nH^0\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi,\\mathcal C_Y)\\longrightarrow\\mathcal A_E\n\\right].\n\\]</p><p>**Statement.**\nFor every closed invariant \\(Y\\subset\\widehat{\\mathcal N}\\), the natural map\n\\[\nE\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\n\\bigl(\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb Q}\\cap\\mathcal F_Y\\mathcal A_E\\bigr)\n\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\n\\mathcal F_Y\\mathcal A_E\n\\]\nis an isomorphism, with the intersection taken inside \\(\\mathcal A_E\\).</p><p>Moreover, if \\(E_\\ell=\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_\\ell\\) and the closed supports \\(Y_\\ell\\subset\\widehat{\\mathcal N}_{E_\\ell}\\) have the same nilpotent-orbit labels under the pinned split dual group, then\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb Q}\\cap\\mathcal F_{Y_\\ell}\\mathcal A_{E_\\ell}\n=\n\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb Q}\\cap\\mathcal F_{Y_{\\ell'}}\\mathcal A_{E_{\\ell'}}\n\\qquad\n(\\ell,\\ell'\\ne\\operatorname{char}(\\mathbb F_q)).\n\\]\nNo Hecke-finiteness or cuspidality condition is imposed, and \\(X(\\mathbb F_q)\\) need not be nonempty.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 12,\n\\[\nW=\\mathcal F_Y\\mathcal A_E\n\\]\nis invariant under all coefficient automorphisms over \\(\\mathbb Q\\). Apply Proposition 13 to \\(F=E\\) and \\(V_{\\mathbb Q}=\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb Q}\\), with its point-function basis. It gives exactly\n\\[\nE\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\n(\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb Q}\\cap\\mathcal F_Y\\mathcal A_E)\n\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\n\\mathcal F_Y\\mathcal A_E.\n\\]</p><p>The support-trace calculation of Proposition 8 uses all retained fixed components and actual finite combinations of Weil trace classes. Proposition 11 permits every finitely supported test function. Thus the argument applies to the entire function space, including noncuspidal functions and empty or full supports.</p><p>For coefficient-prime independence, choose the same closed point \\(v\\) and abstract isomorphisms\n\\[\nE_\\ell\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\mathbb C\n\\]\nfor all \\(\\ell\\ne p\\). With positive square-root Satake normalization, the moment equations for every pair of functions and the compact sets in Propositions 10–11 are the same for every prime, by the argument of Proposition 12. Character-density uniqueness gives the same transported filtration in \\(\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb C}\\). Intersecting with \\(\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb Q}\\), fixed by every coefficient isomorphism, gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb Q}\\cap\\mathcal F_{Y_\\ell}\\mathcal A_{E_\\ell}\n=\n\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb Q}\\cap\\mathcal F_{Y_{\\ell'}}\\mathcal A_{E_{\\ell'}}.\n\\]\nTheir scalar extensions are the original filtrations by the rationality just proved. This compares rational function subspaces, not spectral categories or individual eigenobjects.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Unramified cuspidal comparison across coefficient primes — original Corollary 7.5.** **Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.** Use the curve \\(X/\\mathbb F_q\\), split connected semisimple group \\(G/\\mathbb F_q\\) …", "statement": "<p>**Unramified cuspidal comparison across coefficient primes — original Corollary 7.5.**</p><p>**Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.**\nUse the curve \\(X/\\mathbb F_q\\), split connected semisimple group \\(G/\\mathbb F_q\\), pinned split rational dual group, and all four characteristic conditions stated in Theorem 1. Put\n\\[\np=\\operatorname{char}(\\mathbb F_q),\\qquad\nE_\\ell=\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_\\ell\\quad(\\ell\\ne p).\n\\]\nAt unramified level \\(D=0\\), let\n\\[\nC_{0,\\mathbb Q}\\subset\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb Q}\n\\]\nbe the cuspidal function space, and let \\(C_{0,\\mathcal O,\\mathbb Q}\\) be its rational orbit summands from [RA, Theorem 1.1]. Set\n\\[\nC_{0,\\ell}=E_\\ell\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}C_{0,\\mathbb Q}.\n\\]</p><p>For each \\(\\ell\\ne p\\), let \\(Y_\\ell\\subset\\widehat{\\mathcal N}_{E_\\ell}\\) be closed and invariant, with the same nilpotent-orbit subset \\(\\mathscr Y\\) under the pinned split rational dual group. Write \\(\\mathbf Y=(Y_\\ell)_{\\ell\\ne p}\\), and define\n\\[\nF^{\\mathbb Q}_{\\mathbf Y,\\ell}\n=\n\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb Q}\\cap\\mathcal F_{Y_\\ell}\\mathcal A_{E_\\ell},\n\\qquad\nV_{\\mathbf Y,\\mathbb Q}\n=\n\\bigoplus_{\\mathcal O\\in\\mathscr Y}C_{0,\\mathcal O,\\mathbb Q}.\n\\]</p><p>**Statement.**\nInside the corresponding finitely supported function spaces,\n\\[\nC_{0,\\mathbb Q}\\cap F^{\\mathbb Q}_{\\mathbf Y,\\ell}\n=\nV_{\\mathbf Y,\\mathbb Q},\n\\qquad\nC_{0,\\ell}\\cap\\mathcal F_{Y_\\ell}\\mathcal A_{E_\\ell}\n=\nE_\\ell\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}V_{\\mathbf Y,\\mathbb Q}.\n\\]\nThe equalities include the zero cusp space and empty or full \\(Y_\\ell\\). This is the unramified cuspidal common-scope form of [GLR, Conjectures 3.4.9 and 3.4.11].</p>", "proof": "<p>For split \\(G\\), the companion’s unramified double quotient is the bundle-class set \\(S\\) [RA, §6.1], giving the point-function embeddings.</p><p>Fix \\(\\ell\\), an abstract \\(\\iota:E_\\ell\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\mathbb C\\), and a closed point \\(v\\), with \\(r=q^{\\deg v}\\). The finite reduced global Hecke image on the cusp space gives finitely many joint eigenspaces [RA, Lemma 6.1]. Restrict to the spherical algebra at \\(v\\), grouping characters with the same one-place Satake class:\n\\[\nC_{0,\\mathbb C}\n=\n\\mathbb C\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}C_{0,\\mathbb Q}\n=\n\\bigoplus_{t\\in\\Sigma_v}C_t,\n\\qquad\nT_V|_{C_t}=\\chi_V(t).\n\\]\nHere \\(t\\) are distinct classes in the positive-\\(\\sqrt r\\) convention and \\(V\\) ranges over finite-dimensional dual-group representations. Proposition 1 gives ordinary Hecke compatibility, and the companion uses volume-one normalization. Square-root corrections are finite central translations [RA, Remark 6.3]; contragredient conventions are inversion. Both preserve every \\(B_{\\mathcal O}\\) by Proposition 9.</p><p>The companion assigns each joint character an orbit label independent of good place and complex embedding, and identifies the complex extension of each rational orbit summand with the sum of joint eigenspaces of that label [RA, Lemma 6.2 and Propositions 6.5–6.6]. Every embedded class of label \\(\\mathcal O\\) lies in \\(B_{\\mathcal O}\\), by the all-embeddings compactness defining \\(R_{r,\\mathcal O}\\). The disjointness in Proposition 9 shows that all joint eigenspaces grouped into one \\(C_t\\) share a label. Hence\n\\[\n\\Sigma_v\\subset B,\\qquad\n\\mathbb C\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}C_{0,\\mathcal O,\\mathbb Q}\n=\n\\bigoplus_{t\\in\\Sigma_v\\cap B_{\\mathcal O}}C_t.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nB_{\\mathbf Y}=\\bigcup_{\\mathcal O\\in\\mathscr Y}B_{\\mathcal O}.\n\\]</p><p>Write \\(f=\\sum_tf_t\\in C_{0,\\mathbb C}\\). For every \\(g\\in\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb C}\\), define the finite atomic measure\n\\[\n\\mu_{f,g}=\\sum_t[f_t,g]\\delta_t.\n\\]\nIt satisfies\n\\[\n[T_Vf,g]\n=\n\\sum_t\\chi_V(t)[f_t,g]\n=\n\\int_B\\chi_V\\,d\\mu_{f,g}.\n\\]\nBy Propositions 9–10, \\(\\mu_{f,g}=\\nu_{f,g}\\).</p><p>If \\(f_t\\ne0\\), choose \\(b\\in S\\) with \\(f_t(b)\\ne0\\), and take \\(g=\\delta_b\\). Then\n\\[\n\\mu_{f,\\delta_b}(\\{t\\})\n=\n[f_t,\\delta_b]\n=\n\\frac{f_t(b)}{|\\operatorname{Aut}(b)|}\\ne0.\n\\]\nDistinct atoms cannot cancel this mass, and the test need not be cuspidal. Thus all detector measures are supported in \\(B_{\\mathbf Y}\\) exactly when \\(f_t=0\\) for every \\(t\\notin B_{\\mathbf Y}\\).</p><p>Proposition 11 and the orbit decomposition give\n\\[\nC_{0,\\mathbb C}\\cap\\iota_*(\\mathcal F_{Y_\\ell}\\mathcal A_{E_\\ell})\n=\n\\mathbb C\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}V_{\\mathbf Y,\\mathbb Q}.\n\\]\nPull back by \\(\\iota\\), which fixes \\(\\mathbb Q\\), to obtain\n\\[\nC_{0,\\ell}\\cap\\mathcal F_{Y_\\ell}\\mathcal A_{E_\\ell}\n=\nE_\\ell\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}V_{\\mathbf Y,\\mathbb Q}.\n\\]\nIntersect with \\(C_{0,\\mathbb Q}\\), using\n\\[\nC_{0,\\mathbb Q}\\cap\n(E_\\ell\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}V_{\\mathbf Y,\\mathbb Q})\n=\nV_{\\mathbf Y,\\mathbb Q},\n\\]\nto obtain the rational equality. Theorem 1 supplies the common coefficient-independent rational filtration in which this comparison takes place.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Established geometric inputs — original Theorems 2.1, 2.3, 2.4, 2.5, 2.6, and Remark 2.2.** **Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.** Use the setting and all four characteristic conditions of Theorem 1.", "statement": "<p>**Established geometric inputs — original Theorems 2.1, 2.3, 2.4, 2.5, 2.6, and Remark 2.2.**</p><p>**Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.**\nUse the setting and all four characteristic conditions of Theorem 1. All categories are \\(E=\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_\\ell\\)-linear. A compact object is one whose Hom functor commutes with filtered colimits.</p><p>**Statement.**\nThe following inputs hold.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. **Restricted geometric Langlands: original Theorem 2.1.** There is a Frobenius-invariant retained union \\(Z\\) of connected components and an equivalence\n   \\[\n   \\mathbb L_G^{\\operatorname{restr}}:\n   \\mathcal C\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\widehat{\\mathcal N}}(Z).\n   \\]\n   It identifies every \\(\\mathcal C_Y\\) with \\(\\operatorname{IndCoh}_Y(Z)\\), intertwines Hecke functors with tensoring by evaluation bundles, and carries \\(\\Phi\\) to \\(f^!\\), where \\(f\\) pulls a local system back along geometric Frobenius of \\(\\overline X\\). The embedding\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C\\hookrightarrow\n   \\operatorname{Shv}(\\operatorname{Bun}_G(\\overline X),E)\n   \\]\n   preserves compact objects. The retained prime union is used throughout; its identification with the entire restricted stack is not needed.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. **Theta normalization: original Remark 2.2.** The curve \\(X\\) admits a line bundle \\(\\vartheta\\), defined over \\(\\mathbb F_q\\), with\n   \\[\n   \\vartheta^{\\otimes2}\\simeq\\omega_X.\n   \\]\n   Thus the theta normalization of the equivalence can be made over \\(\\mathbb F_q\\), without requiring a rational point.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. **Trace and ordinary local terms: original Theorem 2.3.** There is a canonical isomorphism of complexes\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{LT}:\n   \\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi,\\mathcal C)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\mathcal A_E,\n   \\]\n   with the right side in degree zero. For compact \\(M\\) and \\(b:M\\to\\Phi M\\), it sends \\([M,b]\\) to the ordinary alternating trace function of the adjoint morphism\n   \\[\n   (\\operatorname{Frob}_{\\operatorname{Bun}})^*M\\to M.\n   \\]\n   This function has finite support. The comparison is compatible with Hecke operations, pullback, star tensor product, and compactly supported pushforward.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. **Nonstandard duality: original Theorem 2.4.** The pairing\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal C\\otimes\\mathcal C\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Vect}_E,\n   \\qquad\n   (M,N)\\longmapsto\n   R\\Gamma_c\\!\\left(\\operatorname{Bun}_G(\\overline X),\n   M\\overset{*}{\\otimes}N\\right)\n   \\]\n   defines a self-duality invariant under simultaneous Frobenius. On compact inputs its value is perfect, also after any finite Hecke representation is inserted. On compact Weil objects its numerical trace is the bilinear pairing\n   \\[\n   [u,g]=\\sum_{b\\in S}\\frac{u(b)g(b)}{|\\operatorname{Aut}(b)|}.\n   \\]\n   Compact objects of the categorical dual are opposites of compact objects of \\(\\mathcal C\\), with evaluation given by enriched Hom. For\n   \\[\n   a:\\Phi M\\to M,\\qquad b:N\\to\\Phi N,\n   \\]\n   the induced endomorphism of \\(\\operatorname{Hom}(M,N)\\) is\n   \\[\n   h\\longmapsto\\Phi^{-1}(b\\circ h\\circ a).\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">5. **Components and deformation theory: original Theorem 2.5.** Every connected component \\(Z_\\sigma\\) of the restricted local-system stack has a unique semisimple isomorphism class \\(\\sigma\\). Its automorphism group\n   \\[\n   J=\\operatorname{Aut}(\\sigma)\n   \\]\n   is reductive, possibly disconnected. There is a formal affine coarse space \\(S_\\sigma\\) with reduced space a point, and \\(Z_\\sigma\\to S_\\sigma\\) is relatively algebraic. After framing at a geometric point, the component is formal affine. Base change along an affine infinitesimal stage \\(S_n\\to S_\\sigma\\) is affine; almost-finite-type stages and bounded Postnikov truncations may be used, with coherent cohomology in bounded ranges. At a local system \\(\\tau\\),\n   \\[\n   T_\\tau\\operatorname{LocSys}^{\\operatorname{restr}}_{\\widehat G}(\\overline X)\n   \\simeq R\\Gamma(\\overline X,\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}_\\tau)[1].\n   \\]\n   In particular the stack is quasi-smooth.</p><p>   For \\(d\\) frames, put \\(K=\\widehat G^d\\). The unique closed \\(K\\)-orbit is \\(K/J\\), and every nonempty invariant closed subset of a finite stage meets that orbit.</p><p class=\"li num\">6. **Pure Weil lift: original Theorem 2.6.** Fix an abstract field isomorphism\n   \\[\n   \\iota:E\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\mathbb C.\n   \\]\n   If \\(Z_\\sigma\\) is Frobenius invariant, then \\(\\sigma\\) admits an \\(\\iota\\)-pure weight-zero Weil structure. Every Frobenius eigenvalue on\n   \\[\n   H^i(\\overline X,\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}_\\sigma)\n   \\]\n   has \\(\\iota\\)-absolute value \\(q^{i/2}\\), for \\(0\\le i\\le2\\). No semisimplicity of these Frobenius operators is asserted.</p><p>   Writing\n   \\[\n   \\mathfrak j=\\operatorname{Lie}(J),\\qquad\n   V=H^1(\\overline X,\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}_\\sigma),\n   \\]\n   curve duality and an invariant form give\n   \\[\n   H^0(\\overline X,\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}_\\sigma)=\\mathfrak j,\n   \\qquad\n   H^2(\\overline X,\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}_\\sigma)\\simeq\\mathfrak j^*(-1),\n   \\]\n   and a \\(J\\)-invariant symplectic form\n   \\[\n   V\\otimes V\\to E(-1).\n   \\]\n   After forgetting its Tate factor, the cup–bracket obstruction is the quadratic moment map\n   \\[\n   \\mu:V\\to\\mathfrak j^*,\n   \\qquad\n   \\langle\\mu(x),z\\rangle=\\tfrac12\\omega(zx,x).\n   \\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The characteristic assumptions are precisely those specified in [AGKRRV1, §14.4.1 and §D.1.1].</p><p>For restricted geometric Langlands, the equivalence and preservation of compact objects are [GR, Theorems 1.3.9(i) and 1.1.7]; Frobenius invariance is [GR, §1.5.4]. Hecke compatibility is [GR, Lemma 1.3.7], support compatibility is [Ras, Theorem 4.1.1 and Corollary 4.2.1], and the Frobenius convention is [GLR, §1.3.4]. The companion [RGL, Theorem 1.4, regime A] identifies the retained union with the full restricted stack under the same assumptions, but that identification is not required here.</p><p>At a geometric point \\(x\\), a representation \\(D\\) supplies the bundle \\(\\sigma\\mapsto D_{\\sigma,x}\\). Above a closed point of degree \\(d\\), the geometric points form a Frobenius cycle of length \\(d\\); the Hecke construction uses all \\(d\\) evaluation bundles with cyclic transport.</p><p>For theta normalization, in odd characteristic [Ati, §5, Remark 2 after Proposition 5.2] gives a Frobenius-invariant square-root class. The Brauer group of a finite field vanishes, so the class descends. In characteristic two, choose \\(a\\in\\mathbb F_q(X)\\) with \\(da\\ne0\\). Laurent expansion over each perfect residue field shows that all orders of \\(da\\) are even. Hence\n\\[\n\\mathcal O_X\\!\\left(\\tfrac12\\operatorname{div}(da)\\right)\n\\]\nis a square root of \\(\\omega_X\\), as in [Mum, §4].</p><p>The trace isomorphism is [AGKRRV3, Theorem 0.2.6, equivalently Corollary 4.1.4]. Its ordinary local-term identification is [AGKRRV3, Theorem 0.6.8, equivalently Theorem 5.2.3, and Remark 0.6.5]. The compact-embedding hypothesis is supplied by restricted geometric Langlands. The compatibilities and stack summation convention are recalled in [GLR, §1.1]. This applies to all finitely supported functions.</p><p>The duality is [AGKRRV2, Theorem 3.2.2]. For its finiteness assertion, compact inputs are compact in ambient sheaves and hence constructible [AGKRRV2, Appendix A.1.6]. Star tensoring an ambient compact with a constructible object preserves compactness. For \\(p:\\operatorname{Bun}_G\\to\\operatorname{pt}\\), the functor \\(p_!\\) is left adjoint to the continuous functor \\(p^!\\), so preserves compactness [AGKRRV2, Appendix A.1.7]. Its value is therefore compact in \\(\\operatorname{Vect}_E\\), hence perfect. A finite Hecke functor has a continuous adjoint supplied by the dual representation and likewise preserves compactness. Frobenius invariance follows by change of variables. The numerical pairing is the Grothendieck trace formula with groupoid counting measure. The opposite-category description and enriched-Hom evaluation are [AGKRRV2, §§0.5.3 and 5.1.2]; reversing the Weil arrow gives the displayed Hom action.</p><p>The component and finiteness assertions assemble [AGKRRV1, Theorems 1.4.5 and 1.6.3, Remarks 1.4.6–1.4.7, Corollary 3.7.4, Theorem 5.4.2, Corollary 5.4.6, and §7.9.10]. The tangent formula is [AGKRRV1, Proposition 2.2.2 and §§2.4, 25.4.3]. Formal quasi-smoothness is [AGKRRV1, §§21.2.1–21.2.3]; the required amplitude follows from the curve’s cohomological dimension two. Semisimplicity makes geometric monodromy reductive, and its centralizer is reductive in characteristic zero. Adding frames is an affine torsor operation. Closed framed orbits correspond to semisimple local systems, with stabilizer \\(J\\), proving the closed-orbit assertions.</p><p>The pure-lift application to a fixed semisimple point is stated in [GLR, Appendix A.5.4–A.5.5], using [AGKRRV1, Corollary 3.7.4 and Proposition 25.4.6]. The cited appendix records the semisimple consequence of the irreducible-parameter statement. Purity of \\(H^1\\) is the smooth proper curve application of Weil II [Del, Corollary 3.3.6 and §3.3.10]. Purity of \\(H^0\\) follows by viewing horizontal sections in a pure fiber, and purity of \\(H^2\\) follows by curve duality. The invariant form and duality give the final identifications and symplectic moment-map data.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**The framed quadratic model — original Theorem 3.4.** **Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.** Use the global setting of Theorem 1 and fix \\(\\iota:E\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\mathbb C\\); transport coefficients to …", "statement": "<p>**The framed quadratic model — original Theorem 3.4.**</p><p>**Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.**\nUse the global setting of Theorem 1 and fix \\(\\iota:E\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\mathbb C\\); transport coefficients to \\(\\mathbb C\\). All absolute values in this proposition refer to \\(\\iota\\). An operator has absolute weight \\(w\\) if every eigenvalue has modulus \\(q^{w/2}\\); this does not imply semisimplicity.</p><p>Fix a Frobenius-invariant retained component \\(Z_\\sigma\\), choose the pure Weil lift of Proposition 1, and put\n\\[\nJ=\\operatorname{Aut}(\\sigma),\\qquad\n\\mathfrak j=\\operatorname{Lie}(J),\\qquad\nV=H^1(\\overline X,\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}_\\sigma).\n\\]\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the symplectic form after forgetting the Tate factor, and set\n\\[\n\\mu:V\\to\\mathfrak j^*,\n\\qquad\n\\langle\\mu(x),z\\rangle=\\tfrac12\\omega(zx,x).\n\\]\nThe zero fiber \\(\\mu^{-1}(0)\\) always means the derived zero fiber.</p><p>Choose a closed point \\(v\\) of degree \\(d\\) and frames at its \\(d\\) geometric points, and write \\(K=\\widehat G^d\\). Let \\(\\operatorname{unst}\\subset V\\) be the inverse image of the origin under \\(V\\to V/\\!/J\\).</p><p>**Statement.**\nThere is a model, compatible with Frobenius, the evaluation bundles at the cyclic tuple above \\(v\\), and the singular-support map,\n\\[\nZ_\\sigma\\simeq\n\\left[(\\mu^{-1}(0))^\\wedge_{\\operatorname{unst}}/J\\right].\n\\]\nIn this model:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The singularity scheme has points \\((x,z)\\) satisfying\n   \\[\n   \\mu(x)=0,\\qquad zx=0,\\qquad z\\in\\mathfrak j.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The support condition defined by \\(Y\\subset\\widehat{\\mathcal N}\\) is that the image of \\(z\\) under \\(\\mathfrak j\\hookrightarrow\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}\\) belong to \\(Y\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Every evaluation representation of \\(K\\) is the bundle associated to its restriction along \\(J\\hookrightarrow K\\), and Frobenius has constant cyclic transport on these bundles.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. After removing the scalars \\(q^{1/2}\\) on deformation points and \\(q^{-1}\\) on singular points, Frobenius is a linear automorphism of the moment-map data, normalizing \\(J\\) and the framed representation labels. Its eigenvalues on these normalized linear data have modulus one.</p><p>Completion is along the invariant null fiber, not merely along the closed orbit.</p>", "proof": "<p>Work first in the completion of the framed component along \\(K/J\\). The smooth affine orbit admits an equivariant formal retraction: lift the identity retraction through successive nilpotent neighborhoods using smoothness, and average the lifting torsors using reductivity. Its fiber is a formal slice with stabilizer \\(J\\), and the framed completion is its induction from \\(J\\) to \\(K\\).</p><p>The transverse tangent and obstruction spaces are\n\\[\nV=H^1(\\overline X,\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}_\\sigma),\n\\qquad\nH^2(\\overline X,\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}_\\sigma).\n\\]\nQuasi-smoothness gives a minimal presentation by complete free coordinates dual to these spaces in degrees \\(0,-1\\), obtained by removing contractible linear pairs in a free resolution. The splittings may be chosen \\(J\\)-equivariantly.</p><p>To identify the quadratic obstruction, use the simplicial descent model for local systems underlying the tangent formula of Proposition 1, computed by hypercover cochains with the \\(\\ell\\)-adic limit retained. Write a perturbation modulo \\(t^3\\) as\n\\[\ng_{ij}\\exp(tx_{ij}+t^2y_{ij}).\n\\]\nThe Baker–Campbell–Hausdorff expansion of the cocycle equation gives\n\\[\ndx=0,\\qquad dy=-\\tfrac12[x,x].\n\\]\nThus the obstruction is the class of \\(\\tfrac12[x,x]\\) in \\(H^2\\). Invariance of the coefficient pairing and graded commutativity give\n\\[\n\\left\\langle\\tfrac12[x,x],z\\right\\rangle\n=\n\\tfrac12\\omega(zx,x)\n=\n\\langle\\mu(x),z\\rangle.\n\\]</p><p>Arrange next that the retraction and coordinates respect the chosen Frobenius transporter. Purity gives absolute point weights \\(0,1,2\\) on orbit tangent, transverse tangent, and obstruction spaces. A positive-transverse-order retraction error has positive source weight and target weight zero; its conjugacy operator has no eigenvalue \\(1\\). At each finite jet it is uniquely removable by the same equation as in Lemma 2.3. Equivariant errors are determined by their \\(J\\)-equivariant values at the base point. Stabilizer transport and cyclic frame permutation have weight zero. This gives a Frobenius-compatible retraction and slice.</p><p>Represent the single cyclic automorphism on the minimal free presentation, using free lifting and reductivity. Its invertible linear part makes it an automorphism of the complete graded algebra; there is no additional relation on an infinite cyclic generator. On coordinate functions use inverse transport, with weights \\(-1,-2\\). Lemma 2.3 linearizes the automorphism and forces the differential to be quadratic. A coordinate change tangent to the identity preserves its quadratic part, so the differential is precisely \\(\\mu\\). Lemma 2.2 restores the invariant base and proves the asserted model.</p><p>For singularities,\n\\[\n\\langle d\\mu_x(v),z\\rangle=\\omega(zx,v).\n\\]\nNondegeneracy identifies \\(\\ker(d\\mu_x^*)\\) with \\(\\{z:zx=0\\}\\). The singular evaluation map is fiberwise linear in \\(z\\); at \\(x=0\\) it is the centralizer inclusion, with its duality Tate factor. Its target singular points have weight \\(-2\\). A correction of positive \\(x\\)-degree \\(n\\), linear in \\(z\\), would have weight \\(n-2\\), so cannot intertwine Frobenius. Finite-jet comparison, valid with Jordan blocks, excludes all such corrections. Changing the invariant form rescales simple factors of \\(\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}\\), preserving nilpotent orbits.</p><p>A \\(K\\)-frame trivializes all evaluation bundles. If \\(\\mathscr S\\) is the slice, the retraction identifies the framed completion with \\(K\\times^J\\mathscr S\\), mapping to \\(K/J\\) by \\([k,s]\\mapsto kJ\\). Quotienting the trivial framed bundle associated to a \\(K\\)-representation \\(D\\) gives the bundle associated to \\(D|_J\\). Frobenius compatibility makes its cyclic transport the closed-orbit transport, independent of \\(s\\). Purity gives modulus-one eigenvalues on normalized frame representations; the cyclic action is tested on its \\(d\\)-th power. Together with purity of \\(H^1,H^2\\), this proves all assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Finite covariants and completion — original Lemma 3.1.** Let \\(K\\) be a reductive complex algebraic group, possibly disconnected, and let \\(A\\) be a finitely generated \\(K\\)-algebra.", "statement": "<p>**Finite covariants and completion — original Lemma 3.1.**</p><p>Let \\(K\\) be a reductive complex algebraic group, possibly disconnected, and let \\(A\\) be a finitely generated \\(K\\)-algebra. Suppose \\(A^K\\) is Artinian and \\(\\operatorname{Spec}A\\) has a unique closed \\(K\\)-orbit \\(O\\), with ideal \\(I\\).</p><p>For every equivariant finite \\(A\\)-module \\(M\\) and finite-dimensional \\(K\\)-representation \\(D\\), the inverse system\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_K(D,M/I^nM)\n\\]\nis eventually constant with value \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_K(D,M)\\). Consequently the rational, locally \\(K\\)-finite part of the \\(I\\)-adic completion recovers \\(M\\), and, for \\(M=A\\), recovers \\(A\\) with its multiplication.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a finite-dimensional \\(K\\)-stable generating space \\(U\\subset M\\). The surjection \\(A\\otimes U\\to M\\) induces a surjection on \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_K(D,-)\\), by reductivity. Finite generation of the covariants of \\(A\\) over \\(A^K\\) [Bri, Lemma 2.3] makes\n\\[\nM_D=\\operatorname{Hom}_K(D,M)\n\\]\nfinite over \\(A^K\\), hence finite-dimensional. This also applies to disconnected \\(K\\), since its identity component is reductive and its component group is finite.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nN=\\bigcap_{n\\ge1}I^nM.\n\\]\nIt is a finite equivariant submodule because \\(A\\) is noetherian. At every point of \\(O\\), its localization vanishes by Krull intersection [Sta, Tag 00IP]. If \\(N\\ne0\\), its nonempty invariant closed support would contain a closed orbit and hence meet \\(O\\). Thus \\(N=0\\).</p><p>The descending subspaces \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_K(D,I^nM)\\subset M_D\\) have zero intersection and are eventually zero. Exactness of \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_K(D,-)\\) gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_K(D,M/I^nM)\n=\nM_D/\\operatorname{Hom}_K(D,I^nM).\n\\]\nEvery rational representation is the union of finite-dimensional subrepresentations. Applying the assertion to all \\(D\\) recovers \\(M\\); applying it also to finite tensor products of representation spaces recovers multiplication and every equivariant finite-presentation map.</p><p>For example, with \\(\\mathbb G_m\\)-weights \\(1,-1\\) on \\(x,y\\), set\n\\[\nA_N=\\mathbb C[x,y]/((xy)^N),\\qquad\nA_N^{\\mathbb G_m}=\\mathbb C[t]/(t^N),\\qquad t=xy.\n\\]\nThe locally finite part of the \\((x,y)\\)-adic completion recovers \\(A_N\\), while the ordinary inverse limit also contains non-locally-finite series such as \\(\\sum_{m\\ge0}x^m\\). Rational recovery retains nonzero points on both axes; varying \\(N\\) retains the formal invariant parameter \\(t\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Restoring the invariant base — original Lemma 3.2.** Use the framed component of Proposition 2.", "statement": "<p>**Restoring the invariant base — original Lemma 3.2.**</p><p>Use the framed component of Proposition 2. Its finite affine invariant-base stages have finitely generated classical coordinate rings, Artinian invariant rings, unique closed \\(K\\)-orbit \\(K/J\\), and coherent cohomology in bounded Postnikov ranges, as in Proposition 1.</p><p>An equivariant isomorphism of its completion along \\(K/J\\) with another such completed model extends uniquely over the formal invariant bases to an isomorphism of their invariant-fiber completions. The same holds for equivariant maps, their full mapping spaces, and finite equivariant presentation data. The extension is unique up to a contractible space of choices.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(\\mathcal R_K\\) for the derived category of rational \\(K\\)-representations. All limits below are taken in \\(\\mathcal R_K\\), not among vector spaces with an arbitrary possibly nonrational action.</p><p>First fix an invariant-base stage, with connective coordinate algebra \\(A\\in\\operatorname{CAlg}(\\mathcal R_K)\\). Its hypotheses are:\n\\[\nH^0(A)\\text{ finitely generated},\\qquad H^0(A)^K\\text{ Artinian},\n\\]\na unique closed orbit \\(O\\subset\\operatorname{Spec}H^0(A)\\), and every \\(H^i(A)\\) finite over \\(H^0(A)\\).</p><p>Choose finite-dimensional stable generators for the ideal of \\(O\\), lift them equivariantly to degree-zero cycles, and extend them to stable generators of \\(H^0(A)\\). This gives a polynomial \\(K\\)-algebra \\(P\\to A\\), surjective on \\(H^0\\). If \\(I_P\\) is generated by the chosen orbit equations, put\n\\[\nA_n=A\\otimes_P^{\\mathbf L}P/I_P^n.\n\\]\nThese jets compute completion along \\(O\\); the module comparison is [Sta, Tag 0BKH], and independence of the presentation is [AGKRRV1, Theorem A.1.3(c) and §A.1.5].</p><p>Every \\(H^i(A)\\) is finite over \\(P\\). Noetherian Artin–Rees makes\n\\[\n\\{\\operatorname{Tor}^P_j(H^i(A),P/I_P^n)\\}_n,\\qquad j&gt;0,\n\\]\npro-zero. Since \\(P\\) has finite global dimension, the hyper-Tor calculation has finite width in each cohomological degree, even when \\(A\\) is unbounded below. Hence\n\\[\n\\{H^i(A_n)\\}_n\\simeq\\{H^i(A)/I_P^nH^i(A)\\}_n.\n\\]\nThis is the coherent derived-completion calculation of [Sta, Tags 00MA, 0A06, 0BKH].</p><p>For every finite-dimensional \\(K\\)-representation \\(D\\), Lemma 2.1 and exactness of covariants give\n\\[\n\\{H^iR\\operatorname{Hom}_K(D,A_n)\\}_n\n\\simeq\nH^iR\\operatorname{Hom}_K(D,A),\n\\]\nwith constant right-hand system and zero \\(\\varprojlim^1\\). The functors \\(R\\operatorname{Hom}_K(D,-)\\) commute with limits and jointly detect equivalences. The Milnor exact sequence gives\n\\[\nA\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\operatorname*{holim}_nA_n\n\\quad\\text{in }\\operatorname{CAlg}(\\mathcal R_K).\n\\]\nThe forgetful functor creates limits and detects equivalences, so this is an algebra equivalence, not merely a cohomological identification. Thus, for every rational equivariant coordinate algebra \\(B\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Map}_{\\operatorname{CAlg}(\\mathcal R_K)}(B,A)\n\\simeq\n\\operatorname*{holim}_n\n\\operatorname{Map}_{\\operatorname{CAlg}(\\mathcal R_K)}(B,A_n),\n\\]\ncompatibly with composition. No bound uniform in \\(i\\) or \\(D\\) is used. The same argument applies to modules with coherent cohomology and recovers finite module presentations, maps, relations, and coherent homotopies.</p><p>Next recover the invariant base. Its formal coarse presentation is constructed from the system of trivial covariants of the framed affine stages [AGKRRV1, §§5.3.5–5.3.6]. The trivial case of the preceding calculation recovers this entire system; an arbitrarily chosen base stage is not identified with the spectrum of its pullback’s invariant algebra. On classical invariant functions the topologies are cofinal: the invariant ideal of definition \\(\\mathfrak m\\) lies in \\(I\\), and after fixing a nilpotent invariant-base stage, Lemma 2.1 makes the trivial covariants of sufficiently high powers of \\(I\\) vanish. Apply this in each bounded range for the derived systems. An equivariant map of orbit completions therefore recovers the formal base map.</p><p>Finally, use the noetherian formal-affine presentation of the base supplied by [AGKRRV1, Theorem A.1.3(b),(c) and Remark A.1.4]. Choose finitely many invariant generators \\(t_1,\\ldots,t_r\\) of an ideal of definition. Its infinitesimal stages may be taken to be the derived Koszul thickenings\n\\[\nR_N=R//(t_1^N,\\ldots,t_r^N)\n=\nR\\otimes_{\\mathbb C[u_1,\\ldots,u_r]}^{\\mathbf L}\\mathbb C,\n\\qquad\nu_j\\mapsto t_j^N,\n\\]\nas in [AGKRRV1, §7.1 and §§7.9.8–7.9.10].</p><p>Restrict a map of orbit completions to one affine source stage \\(A\\). The mapping-space equivalence recovers its base map \\(R\\to A\\), with homotopies. The images of \\(t_j\\) are nilpotent in the Artinian algebra \\(H^0(A)^K\\). Choose a common \\(N\\) and invariant nullhomotopies of \\(t_j^N\\); these exist because zero in \\(H^0\\) is a boundary, and reductivity makes the choices invariant. The universal property factors the base map through \\(R_N\\). Pulling back the target gives an affine target stage, with coordinate algebra \\(B_N\\). The completed-stage map extends uniquely by the displayed mapping-space equivalence with \\(B=B_N\\). Nilpotence alone would not justify factoring through an ordinary quotient.</p><p>The Koszul stages are cofinal tests for the formal neighborhood. Above a fixed map with nilpotent \\(t_j\\), differences of nullhomotopies are additive loop spaces; passage from \\(N\\) to \\(M\\) multiplies differences by \\(t_j^{M-N}\\). Nilpotence eventually kills every such homotopy group. The nonempty filtered space of choices is therefore contractible, without interchanging a filtered colimit with a Postnikov limit. The recovered maps are compatible across source stages. Applying the argument to inverse maps and compositions proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Nonresonance with Jordan blocks — original Lemma 3.3.** Let a minimal complete free graded-commutative algebra have generators \\(V^*\\) in cohomological degree \\(0\\) and \\(R^*\\) in degree \\(-1\\).", "statement": "<p>**Nonresonance with Jordan blocks — original Lemma 3.3.**</p><p>Let a minimal complete free graded-commutative algebra have generators \\(V^*\\) in cohomological degree \\(0\\) and \\(R^*\\) in degree \\(-1\\). Suppose a continuous graded automorphism has linear parts of absolute weights \\(-1\\) and \\(-2\\), respectively.</p><p>It is conjugate, by a formal graded coordinate change tangent to the identity, to its linear part. If it commutes with a minimal differential, then in the resulting coordinates the differential sends \\(R^*\\) to quadratic polynomials in \\(V^*\\). These assertions hold equivariantly for a reductive group normalized by the automorphism.</p>", "proof": "<p>Filter coordinate changes by total generator order. At order \\(n\\ge2\\), the error in a degree-zero generator belongs to\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}(V^*,\\operatorname{Sym}^nV^*).\n\\]\nThe conjugacy operator “linear target action times inverse linear source action” has eigenvalues of modulus \\(q^{-(n-1)/2}\\), hence none equal to \\(1\\).</p><p>An error in a degree-minus-one generator contains exactly one degree-minus-one factor and \\(n-1\\) degree-zero factors. The operator on\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}(R^*,R^*\\otimes\\operatorname{Sym}^{n-1}V^*)\n\\]\nhas the same modulus. Its difference from the identity is invertible on generalized eigenspaces; diagonalization is unnecessary. Solve the conjugacy equation at each order. The corrections converge in the generator-adic topology.</p><p>In the equivariant case, the errors and corrections lie in equivariant subspaces preserved by the operator and its inverse.</p><p>Transport the differential. Degree forces it to vanish on degree-zero generators. Minimality excludes constant and linear terms on degree-minus-one generators. Since it commutes with the linearized automorphism, an order-\\(n\\) term can occur only if weight \\(-n\\) agrees with \\(-2\\). Only \\(n=2\\) remains.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Inverse Hom transport and simultaneous compact normalization — original Lemmas 3.5 and 3.6.** **Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.** Use the fixed complex coefficient identification and quadratic component …", "statement": "<p>**Inverse Hom transport and simultaneous compact normalization — original Lemmas 3.5 and 3.6.**</p><p>**Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.**\nUse the fixed complex coefficient identification and quadratic component model of Proposition 2. For compact objects choose structures\n\\[\n\\alpha_M:\\Phi M\\xrightarrow{\\sim}M.\n\\]\nOn \\(h:M\\to N\\), define\n\\[\nT(h)=\\alpha_N\\Phi(h)\\alpha_M^{-1},\n\\qquad\nS(h)=\\Phi^{-1}(\\alpha_N^{-1}h\\alpha_M)=T^{-1}(h).\n\\]</p><p>Include in the normalized linear data a faithful representation of \\(\\widehat G\\) at all \\(d\\) framed points, together with \\(V\\) and \\(\\mathfrak j\\). Let \\(J.e\\subset\\mathfrak j\\) be a nilpotent orbit preserved by the normalized transporter.</p><p>**Statement.**</p><p class=\"li num\">1. **Original Lemma 3.5.** The bilinear Hom action is \\(S\\). It gives absolute weight \\(-1\\) to deformation functions and \\(+2\\) to degree-two obstruction operators. Associated point weights on \\((x,z)\\) are \\((1,-2)\\), so\n   \\[\n   W(x,z)=\\langle z,\\mu(x)\\rangle\n   \\]\n   has weight zero.</p><p>   After identifying the original \\(\\Phi\\)-graph bimodule with the graph of transported arrow action, simultaneous bar actions \\(T,S\\) are inverse. If\n   \\[\n   bH+Hb=1-T,\n   \\]\n   then\n   \\[\n   b(-T^{-1}H)+(-T^{-1}H)b=1-S.\n   \\]\n   Thus contracting \\(S\\)-weights does not replace \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi,-)\\) by \\(\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi^{-1},-)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. **Original Lemma 3.6.** After changing the normalized transporter by an element of \\(J\\), choose a triple \\((e,h,f)\\) and write that transporter as\n   \\[\n   u=tv_0.\n   \\]\n   There are simultaneous compact forms of group and frame data such that:</p><p class=\"li l2\">\\(t\\) belongs to a compact group, preserves \\(J_{\\mathrm{cpt}}\\), and centralizes the triple;</p><p class=\"li l2\">\\(v_0\\) is unipotent, centralizes \\(J\\), and commutes with \\(t\\);</p><p class=\"li l2\">for \\(L_e=Z_J(\\operatorname{SL}_{2,e})\\),\n     \\[\n     L_{e,\\mathrm{cpt}}=L_e\\cap J_{\\mathrm{cpt}}\n     \\]\n     is a maximal compact including its components, and every \\(ktv_0\\), \\(k\\in L_{e,\\mathrm{cpt}}\\), has modulus-one eigenvalues on all normalized linear data.</p><p>   The same holds for tensor, symmetric, exterior, subquotient, and triple-weight constructions. After translating by \\(\\lambda_e(\\sqrt q)\\), the semisimple frame value around the \\(d\\)-cycle belongs to\n   \\[\n   \\left\\{\n   [\\lambda_e(q^{d/2})s]:\n   s\\in Z_{\\widehat G}(\\operatorname{SL}_{2,e})_{\\mathrm{cpt}}\n   \\right\\}.\n   \\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For the first assertion, Proposition 1 identifies \\(\\Phi\\) with \\(f^!\\), which is usual pullback because \\(f\\) is an automorphism. A covariant trace structure is \\(b:N\\to\\Phi N\\), while the opposite input has \\(a:\\Phi M\\to M\\). Their pairing sends\n\\[\nh\\longmapsto\\Phi^{-1}(bha).\n\\]\nWith \\(b=\\alpha_N^{-1}\\) and \\(a=\\alpha_M\\), this is \\(S=T^{-1}\\).</p><p>For the signs, take a coordinate and relation generator with\n\\[\nf^\\#x=a_1x,\\qquad f^\\#\\epsilon=a_2\\epsilon,\\qquad |x|=0,\\quad|\\epsilon|=-1.\n\\]\nInverse transport sends multiplication by \\(x\\) to multiplication by \\(a_1^{-1}x\\). The degree-two Koszul operator dual to \\(\\epsilon\\) transforms by \\(a_2\\). Purity gives \\(|a_1|=q^{1/2}\\), \\(|a_2|=q\\), yielding the stated weights, also on generalized eigenspaces. Point weights are contragredient.</p><p>A raw left-graph coefficient is \\(m\\in\\operatorname{Hom}(M,\\Phi N)\\), identified with an ordinary arrow by \\(m\\mapsto\\alpha_Nm\\). Its simultaneous forward operator is\n\\[\nm\\longmapsto\\Phi(\\alpha_N)\\Phi(m)\\alpha_M^{-1},\n\\]\nnot the ordinary-arrow formula. Under the graph identification this becomes transported ordinary-arrow action. Multiplying the paracyclic identity by \\(-T^{-1}\\) gives the displayed identity for \\(S\\). The full graph identification, including permuted labels and the last face, is retained in Lemma 8.1.</p><p>For compact normalization, write the Jordan decomposition of the original normalized transporter as\n\\[\nu_1=s_1v_1.\n\\]\nThe semisimple part has compact closure of its powers in the faithful simultaneous realization.</p><p>For a complex reductive group, including disconnected groups,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Aut}(J)^\\circ\n=\n\\operatorname{im}\\bigl(J^\\circ\\to\\operatorname{Aut}(J)\\bigr).\n\\]\nIndeed, an infinitesimal automorphism is inner on the semisimple part of \\(J^\\circ\\), and trivial on its central torus, whose character-lattice automorphism group is discrete. After subtracting that inner action, values on other components give a cocycle of the finite group \\(\\pi_0(J)\\) in \\(\\operatorname{Lie}Z(J^\\circ)\\). Averaging makes it a coboundary, hence an inner infinitesimal action by the central torus.</p><p>The kernel of \\(J^\\circ\\to\\operatorname{Aut}(J)\\) is diagonalizable and central. The automorphism induced by \\(v_1\\) therefore has a unique unipotent lift \\(a\\in J^\\circ\\): take the unipotent part of a lift; two such lifts differ by a central semisimple element. Since \\(s_1\\) commutes with \\(v_1\\), uniqueness gives \\(s_1as_1^{-1}=a\\). Set\n\\[\nv_0=a^{-1}v_1.\n\\]\nIt is unipotent, centralizes \\(J\\), and commutes with \\(s_1\\). Here \\(v_1av_1^{-1}=a\\), since \\(v_1\\) acts on \\(J\\) by conjugation by \\(a\\). Changing the transporter by \\(a^{-1}\\) leaves \\(s_1v_0\\). The unipotent part acts innerly on \\(\\mathfrak j\\), so \\(s_1\\) preserves the stable orbit.</p><p>Let \\(A_1\\) be the Zariski closure of \\(\\langle s_1\\rangle\\). It is diagonalizable, normalizes \\(J\\), and \\(M=JA_1\\) is reductive: its unipotent radical maps trivially to \\(M/J\\) and would be a connected normal unipotent subgroup of \\(J^\\circ\\). By the maximal-compact theorem, including disconnected groups [AT, Definition 3.3 and Theorems 3.6–3.7], choose \\(M_{\\mathrm{cpt}}\\) containing the compact closure of \\(\\langle s_1\\rangle\\). Its intersection with \\(J\\) is a maximal compact \\(J_{\\mathrm{cpt}}\\). Averaging a Hermitian form makes the whole compact group unitary on the simultaneous realization.</p><p>Choose the triple so that its \\(\\operatorname{SU}(2)\\) lies in \\(J_{\\mathrm{cpt}}\\). The triple and its \\(s_1\\)-translate are \\(J\\)-conjugate. Their compact restrictions are already conjugate by \\(k_0\\in J_{\\mathrm{cpt}}\\): for a complex conjugator \\(b=kp\\) in polar decomposition, unitarity implies \\(b^*b\\), hence its positive square root \\(p\\), commutes with the first homomorphism; \\(k\\) is the required compact conjugator. Choose its direction so that\n\\[\nt=k_0s_1\n\\]\ncentralizes the triple.</p><p>Since \\(v_0\\) centralizes \\(J\\), it commutes with \\(k_0,t\\), and all \\(k\\in L_{e,\\mathrm{cpt}}\\). The operator \\(kt\\) is unitary; multiplication by commuting unipotent \\(v_0\\) changes neither eigenvalues nor semisimple character. The compact intersection of the triple centralizer is a compact form of its full complex centralizer, including components [AT, Lemma 3.10]. The assertions persist under the indicated functorial constructions.</p><p>Embed the compact frame image in a maximal compact of\n\\[\nK\\rtimes\\langle\\text{cyclic permutation}\\rangle.\n\\]\nIts intersection with \\(K\\) is a product of compact forms. The \\(i\\)-th factor of \\((kt)^d\\) is the compact product transport around the cycle, centralizing the triple in that factor. It is the semisimple part of the corresponding factor of \\((ktv_0)^d\\). Writing \\(w=kt\\),\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\lambda_e(\\sqrt q)wv_0\\bigr)^d\n=\n\\lambda_e(q^{d/2})w^dv_0^d.\n\\]\nNo commutation between \\(k\\) and \\(t\\) is needed. Compact forms of the triple centralizer are conjugate inside it, proving the final assertion. Translation by \\(J\\) changes only the chosen identification of the same Frobenius functor; the further translation fixes \\(e\\), since singular points scale by \\(q^{-1}\\) and \\(e\\) has triple weight two.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Curved localization and orbit generation — original Proposition 4.3, with original Lemmas 4.1 and 4.2.** **Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.** Use the complex quadratic component of Proposition 2 and compact …", "statement": "<p>**Curved localization and orbit generation — original Proposition 4.3, with original Lemmas 4.1 and 4.2.**</p><p>**Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.**\nUse the complex quadratic component of Proposition 2 and compact normalization of Proposition 3. First omit completion along the invariant null fiber. Put\n\\[\nR=\\mathbb C[V],\\qquad\nA=(R\\otimes\\Lambda(\\mathfrak j[1]),d_\\mu),\n\\quad\n|\\eta_a|=-1,\\quad d_\\mu\\eta_a=\\mu_a.\n\\]\nIts curved graded Koszul dual is\n\\[\nB=\\mathbb C[V\\times\\mathfrak j],\n\\qquad |x^*|=0,\\quad |z^*|=2,\\qquad\nW(x,z)=\\langle z,\\mu(x)\\rangle.\n\\]\nA curved module has a degree-one differential \\(\\delta\\) with \\(\\delta^2=W\\).</p><p>For a closed conical support \\(\\Omega\\subset\\mathfrak j\\), write \\(\\mathcal B_\\Omega\\) for the compact curved category with \\(z\\)-support in \\(\\Omega\\), and \\(\\mathcal B_{\\Omega,0}\\) when ordinary support over the invariant origin in \\(\\operatorname{Spec}R^J\\) is also imposed. Compact quotients are idempotent completed.</p><p>The intersection \\(\\mathfrak j\\cap\\widehat{\\mathcal N}\\) is the nilpotent cone of \\(\\mathfrak j\\), with finitely many \\(J\\)-orbits. Fix \\(O=J.e\\) and an \\(\\mathfrak{sl}_2\\)-triple \\((e,h,f)\\). Set\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\lambda_e,\\qquad\nC_e=J^e=L_e\\ltimes U_e,\\qquad\nL_e=Z_J(\\operatorname{SL}_{2,e}),\\qquad\nN_e=\\mathfrak j/[\\mathfrak j,e].\n\\]\nSubscripts \\(i\\) denote \\(h\\)-weights. The zero orbit has trivial triple and \\(U_e=1\\).</p><p>For a stable orbit, translate the normalized inverse action by \\(\\lambda(\\sqrt q)\\) to fix \\(e\\), and call it \\(S_e\\). For orbits or labels stable only under a power, use the same convention for that power. Lift the original grading, acting on \\(z\\)-points by \\(t^{-2}\\), by \\(\\lambda(t)\\). The resulting lifted degrees and absolute \\(S_e\\)-weights are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{factor}&amp;\\text{lifted degree before Ext shift}&amp;\\text{absolute }S_e\\text{-weight}\\\\ \\hline\n(V_i)^*&amp;-i&amp;-1-i\\\\\n(N_e)_i&amp;i-2&amp;-2+i\\\\\n\\operatorname{Lie}(U_e)_i^*&amp;-i&amp;-i.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe original parity differs from lifted-degree parity by \\(\\lambda(-1)\\); polynomial coordinates remain commuting even in odd lifted degree.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nQ_e(x)=W(x,e),\\qquad\nD_e=\\operatorname{Cl}(V_{-1},Q_e),\n\\]\nwith \\(c(v)^2=Q_e(v)\\), and let \\(\\mathcal E_e\\) be the category of finite-dimensional \\(D_e\\)-modules with compatible algebraic \\(L_e\\)-action. For \\(P\\in\\mathcal E_e\\), place its coefficient space in lifted degree zero and give it original parity\n\\[\n\\gamma_P=\\rho_P(\\lambda(-1)),\n\\qquad\n\\gamma_Pc(v)\\gamma_P^{-1}=-c(v).\n\\]\nDefine \\(M_P\\) over \\(e\\) as the curved pushforward from \\(V_{\\ge-1}\\subset V\\) of\n\\[\n\\left(\\mathcal O_{V_{\\ge-1}}\\otimes P,\\ \\delta=c(x_{-1})\\right),\n\\]\nand descend equivariantly over \\(J.e\\), then push into an open where the orbit is closed.</p><p>**Statement.**\nThe objects \\(M_P\\), for simple \\(P\\in\\mathcal E_e\\), generate the compact category supported on \\(J.e\\) in such an open. Generation allows finite cones, shifts, and summands. The category \\(\\mathcal E_e\\) is semisimple.</p>", "proof": "<p>The quadratic form \\(Q_e|_{V_{-1}}\\) is nondegenerate: the map \\(e:V_{-1}\\to V_1\\) is an isomorphism, and the symplectic form identifies \\(V_1\\) with \\(V_{-1}^*\\). The unipotent radical acts trivially on the coefficient quotient and on the projection \\(V_{\\ge-1}\\to V_{-1}\\). On \\(V_{\\ge-1}\\), only two inputs of weight \\(-1\\) contribute to \\(Q_e\\), so the specified differential squares to \\(Q_e\\). The grading and equivariance descend as stated.</p><p>For a locally free factorization,\n\\[\n\\delta(\\partial_v\\delta)+(\\partial_v\\delta)\\delta=\\partial_vQ_e.\n\\]\nThus the derivatives annihilate the identity up to homotopy. Their common zero locus is \\(\\ker(e:V\\to V)\\), consisting of highest-weight vectors and lying in \\(V_{\\ge0}\\). Lemma 4.2 reduces generation to curved pushforwards from \\(V_{\\ge0}\\), where the potential is zero.</p><p>Finite graded free modules on this affine space, with finite stabilizer-representation labels and shifts, generate. For the nonreductive stabilizer, every finite rational \\(C_e\\)-module has a finite filtration with \\(U_e\\)-trivial subquotients, obtained by successively taking unipotent invariants and retaining \\(L_e\\)-stability. Rational \\(U_e\\)-cohomology has dimension bound \\(\\dim U_e\\) and is computed by its finite Lie-cochain complex. Equivariant free tests detect underlying graded cohomology by tensoring with all finite representation labels, passing to their union in the regular representation, and then taking invariants. These operations preserve colimits, so the tests are compact generators.</p><p>No additional curved zero-potential object is invisible to these tests. After finite projective replacement, an acyclic folded differential module over the regular polynomial ring is absolutely acyclic: resolve cycles and boundaries by finite projective resolutions and fold the resulting bounded exact complexes. Equivariant descent adds only the bounded Lie-cochain complex. Equivalently, nonpositive lifted coordinate degrees on \\(V_{\\ge0}\\) permit filtering finite free representatives by generator degree, reducing to finite complexes over the degree-zero polynomial ring.</p><p>Stabilizing the zero section of \\(V_{-1}\\) replaces such a generator by \\(M_P\\) with the regular Clifford module. In coordinates, the Koszul differential for \\(x_{-1}=0\\) and its Clifford partner square to \\(Q_e(x_{-1})\\). The normal coordinates have lifted degree one, so Koszul shifts place the coefficient module in lifted degree zero. Tensoring with \\(L_e\\)-representations and allowing shifts gives the preceding generators.</p><p>Finally, \\(D_e\\) is finite-dimensional semisimple, including in odd dimension. Average a \\(D_e\\)-linear splitting over a compact form of \\(L_e\\). Conjugation preserves \\(D_e\\)-linearity, so the averaged splitting is \\(L_e\\)-equivariant. This proves semisimplicity, including the finite component group, and reduces the generating list to simple labels.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Koszul model and support — original Lemma 4.1.** Use \\(A,B,W,R,J\\) from Proposition 4.", "statement": "<p>**Koszul model and support — original Lemma 4.1.**</p><p>Use \\(A,B,W,R,J\\) from Proposition 4. The compact ind-coherent category on the uncompleted quotient\n\\[\n[\\mu^{-1}(0)/J]\n\\]\nis the category of finite coherent graded \\(J\\)-equivariant factorizations of \\(W\\) over \\(B\\). Coherent singular support in a closed cone \\(\\Omega\\subset\\mathfrak j\\) corresponds to local vanishing of the factorization off \\(V\\times\\Omega\\).</p><p>The completion in Proposition 2 additionally imposes ordinary support on the inverse image of \\(0\\in\\operatorname{Spec}R^J\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The finite-type derived quotient is QCA. Its compact ind-coherent objects are coherent by [DG, Theorem 3.3.5].</p><p>A coherent \\(A\\)-module is perfect as an \\(R\\)-complex because \\(R\\) is regular. Choose a bounded finite projective \\(R\\)-resolution and transfer the degree-minus-one generator actions, allowing higher homotopies. The Koszul twisting cochain on the symmetric divided-power coalgebra of the dual generators turns these homotopies into a differential on its extension to \\(B\\), with square\n\\[\n\\sum_a z_a\\mu_a.\n\\]\nLinear coefficients encode nullhomotopies of \\(\\mu_a\\); higher coefficients encode compatibility homotopies. Conversely, coefficients of the curved differential specialized at \\(z=0\\) recover the homotopy-coherent \\(A\\)-action.</p><p>The free Koszul resolution proves the constructions inverse on modules and morphisms: the exterior-length filtration gives ordinary exterior/symmetric Koszul equivalence, and the equation differential supplies the curvature. No completed polynomial ring occurs; between bounded finite projective models only finitely many \\(z\\)-monomials occur in a fixed total degree. Differential Hom has square zero because the two curvature terms cancel.</p><p>Equivalently, coherent curved modules are taken modulo totalizations of finite exact sequences. Smoothness of the ambient space gives finite locally free replacements, using curved free covers and partner differentials correcting the square to \\(W\\); underlying resolutions terminate by regularity. Reductivity gives equivariant choices. This is the equivariant derived-zero-locus equivalence of [Tod, Theorem 2.3.3], in the differential graded formulation [PT, Theorem 2.1].</p><p>The \\(z_a\\) are the degree-two complete-intersection operators. Their localization defines singular support. Hence the support category is the kernel of restriction to the complementary open, with the quotient compatibility of [Tod, Proposition 2.3.9]. Support is support of the curved object: a locally free underlying module may vanish in the local curved category.</p><p>For the separate ordinary support condition, [GRo, Proposition 7.4.5] identifies ind-coherent sheaves on formal prestack completion with the kernel of restriction to the complementary open. Its compact objects are the supported coherent objects [Gai, Proposition 4.1.7(b)]. Smooth descent [Gai, Corollary 10.4.5] gives the quotient’s presentable category. If \\(i\\) is the supported inclusion and \\(\\Gamma\\) its continuous right adjoint [Gai, Corollary 4.1.5], then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}(iM,-)=\\operatorname{Hom}(M,\\Gamma(-))\n\\]\nshows that \\(i\\) preserves compactness. Conversely, an ambient compact vanishing on the complement is compact in the supported category because \\(i\\) preserves colimits. This identifies compacts after presentable descent, without commuting compacts with a descent limit.</p><p>Apply this to the invariant null fiber. The completion is the formal prestack, not the spectrum of a completed coordinate ring. This ordinary support condition leaves the singular operators unchanged and can be intersected with the prescribed \\(z\\)-support.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Supported localization — original Lemma 4.2.** Use the curved categories of Proposition 4.", "statement": "<p>**Supported localization — original Lemma 4.2.**</p><p>Use the curved categories of Proposition 4. For closed invariant cones \\(\\Omega'\\subset\\Omega\\), restriction to the complementary open gives a compactly generated localization with compact quotient\n\\[\n\\mathcal B_\\Omega/\\mathcal B_{\\Omega'}.\n\\]\nIf \\(O\\) is an orbit closed in that open, its supported compact category is generated by coherent curved pushforwards from \\(V\\times O\\). Morphisms are computed in the open before derived equivariant sections are taken.</p><p>The same statements hold with invariant null-fiber support.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose finite homogeneous equivariant generators of the relevant ideals on a finite affine cover. Koszul complexes on their powers and their duals give the ordinary local-cohomology projector and complementary localization. They tensor with curved modules because their ordinary differentials square to zero. For a supported compact object, its identity factors through a finite projector stage, making it a summand of such a stage.</p><p>For a smooth closed locus \\(i:Z_0\\hookrightarrow U\\) in the smooth ambient open, let \\(I\\) be its ideal and \\(K_n\\) a finite ordinary perfect projector stage. Its bounded ordinary cohomology sheaves are coherent, supported on \\(Z_0\\), and killed by powers of \\(I\\). Smoothness makes them perfect. Ordinary truncation triangles put \\(K_n\\) in their thick span. Their finite \\(I\\)-adic filtrations have quotients \\(i_*F\\), with \\(F\\) coherent on \\(Z_0\\). These constructions preserve equivariance and grading.</p><p>Ordinary perfect complexes act exactly on curved modules. For a locally free curved representative \\(M\\), the projection formula gives\n\\[\n(i_*F)\\otimes M\\simeq i_*(F\\otimes Li^*M).\n\\]\nThus \\(K_n\\otimes M\\) lies in the thick span of coherent curved pushforwards, and the identity factorization proves generation. Ordinary truncations were applied only to \\(K_n\\), before its exact action on the curved factor.</p><p>The same local-cohomology construction computes restriction and quotient morphisms, with support/quotient compatibility as in [Tod, Proposition 2.3.9]. Finite covers and finite Čech descent compute the ordinary perfect-complex statements. On a homogeneous orbit, equivariant descent is the finite Lie-cochain complex of the unipotent stabilizer followed by exact invariants of the reductive quotient. These bounded operations commute with finite triangles and direct-sum folding. The argument also applies to homogeneous vector bundles and preserves ordinary invariant-base support at every step.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Compact orbit-closure lifts — original Proposition 4.4.** **Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.** Use \\(J,\\mathfrak j,V,W,O=J.e,L_e,D_e,\\mathcal E_e,M_P\\) and the grading conventions of Proposition 4, with the …", "statement": "<p>**Compact orbit-closure lifts — original Proposition 4.4.**</p><p>**Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.**\nUse \\(J,\\mathfrak j,V,W,O=J.e,L_e,D_e,\\mathcal E_e,M_P\\) and the grading conventions of Proposition 4, with the normalized transporter of Proposition 3.</p><p>**Statement.**\nEvery \\(M_P\\) is the restriction of a compact object with \\(z\\)-support in \\(\\overline{J.e}\\). If \\(P\\) has a compatible locally finite cyclic structure, the extension can retain it.</p><p>For a Frobenius-fixed simple label, choose a structure whose triple-translated coefficient eigenvalues have modulus one. The same assertions hold for a power of Frobenius, a finite cyclic list of labels, and after tensoring \\(P\\) by a finite-dimensional \\(L_e\\)-representation with compatible cyclic structure placed in lifted degree zero. Inverse structures are available for opposite-category classes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let\n\\[\nP_e=J_{\\ge0}\n=\n\\{g\\in J:\\lim_{t\\to0}\\lambda(t)g\\lambda(t)^{-1}\\text{ exists}\\},\n\\qquad J_0=Z_J(\\lambda).\n\\]\nConsider the Jacobson–Morozov incidence map\n\\[\n\\pi:J\\times^{P_e}\\mathfrak j_{\\ge2}\\longrightarrow\\overline{J.e},\n\\qquad\n[g,z]\\longmapsto\\operatorname{Ad}(g)z.\n\\]\nIts connected version is the resolution of [Nam, §2.3].</p><p>The \\(\\mathfrak{sl}_2\\)-decomposition gives\n\\[\n[\\mathfrak j_{\\ge0},e]=\\mathfrak j_{\\ge2},\n\\]\nso \\(P_e^\\circ.e\\) is open dense in \\(\\mathfrak j_{\\ge2}\\). Every component of \\(P_e\\) preserves this unique open orbit; hence \\(P_e.e=P_e^\\circ.e\\). Since \\(J^e\\subset P_e\\), the open part of the source is\n\\[\nJ\\times^{P_e}(P_e.e)=J/J^e,\n\\]\nmapping isomorphically to \\(J.e\\). The source is equidimensional of dimension \\(\\dim(J/J^e)\\); its complementary closed subset has smaller dimension and invariant image, so its image cannot meet \\(J.e\\). Thus the displayed open part is the entire inverse image of \\(J.e\\). The quotient \\(J/P_e\\) is a finite disjoint union of flag varieties, and the incidence space is closed in \\((J/P_e)\\times\\mathfrak j\\). Therefore \\(\\pi\\) is proper with image \\(\\overline{J.e}\\).</p><p>The degree-two construction uses the open orbit \\(J_0.e\\subset\\mathfrak j_2\\), with stabilizer \\(L_e\\). The label \\(P\\) gives an equivariant coherent module for the family of Clifford algebras of\n\\[\nQ_z|_{V_{-1}},\\qquad z\\in\\mathfrak j_2.\n\\]\nThis algebra is finite over \\(\\mathcal O_{\\mathfrak j_2}\\), including at degenerate forms. In the quasi-coherent direct image from the open orbit, choose finitely many module generators, enlarge them to a finite-dimensional equivariant space, and take their span under the finite Clifford algebra and polynomial ring. The resulting coherent equivariant submodule restricts to the original family.</p><p>Let \\(t\\) act on this coefficient family through the central element \\(\\lambda(t^{-1})\\in J_0\\). It sends\n\\[\nz\\mapsto t^{-2}z,\n\\qquad\nc_z(w)\\mapsto c_{t^{-2}z}(tw).\n\\]\nWith original \\(x\\)-points fixed, \\(c_z(x_{-1})\\) has degree one. At \\(t=-1\\), coefficient parity is precisely \\(\\lambda(-1)\\), so the extension preserves the original grading.</p><p>Inflate along \\(\\mathfrak j_{\\ge2}\\to\\mathfrak j_2\\) and tensor with \\(\\mathcal O_{V_{\\ge-1}}\\). A term pairing \\(z_k,x_i,x_j\\) in \\(W\\) requires\n\\[\nk+i+j=0,\\qquad k\\ge2,\\qquad i,j\\ge-1,\n\\]\nhence \\(k=2\\), \\(i=j=-1\\). Thus \\(c(x_{-1})^2=W\\) on this locus. The construction is \\(P_e\\)-equivariant because its unipotent radical acts trivially on the two associated graded quotients. Induction and proper pushforward along\n\\[\nJ\\times^{P_e}(V_{\\ge-1}\\times\\mathfrak j_{\\ge2})\n\\longrightarrow V\\times\\mathfrak j\n\\]\ngive the compact coherent factorization. Over \\(J.e\\), it is exactly \\(M_P\\).</p><p>To retain a chosen cyclic operator, take the Zariski closure of the group generated by algebraic equivariance, grading, and that operator; if necessary use a common algebraic cover acting on the coefficient module. Rational direct image is the union of finite-dimensional representations of this group. The finite generating space can therefore be chosen operator-stable, and extension and pushforward retain the structure.</p><p>For a fixed simple module, pairs consisting of a data automorphism and an implementing linear map form an algebraic group over the stabilizer of the label. Its kernel is scalars by Schur’s Lemma. For a lift \\((u,A)\\), take the semisimple Jordan part and its generated diagonalizable group. Characterwise polar decomposition separates positive-real and unit-modulus parts. Since \\(u\\) has modulus-one eigenvalues on faithful normalized data, the positive-real part projects to the identity and hence is \\((1,cI)\\) for \\(c&gt;0\\). All eigenvalues of \\(A\\) have modulus \\(c\\), and \\(c^{-1}A\\) is pure. The Jordan unipotent part gives a compatible commuting unipotent lift.</p><p>Label orbits are finite. Encode the compatible actions as representations of the reductive group \\(D_e^\\times\\rtimes L_e\\). A simple compatible module is irreducible because the units span \\(D_e\\). A connected algebraic family of automorphisms preserves an irreducible class: multiplicities against characters of a fixed maximal compact are continuous integer-valued functions, hence constant; compact Zariski density identifies the algebraic representations. Only the finite component group of the data normalizer can permute labels. Apply the preceding choices to a power or around a finite orbit. Undoing the triple translation gives the original locally finite Weil structure. Tensoring and inversion preserve the construction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Orbit Hom calculation — original Proposition 4.5.** **Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.** Use the orbit category, coefficients, lifted grading, and translated inverse action \\(S_e\\) of Proposition 4.", "statement": "<p>**Orbit Hom calculation — original Proposition 4.5.**</p><p>**Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.**\nUse the orbit category, coefficients, lifted grading, and translated inverse action \\(S_e\\) of Proposition 4. Let \\(P,P'\\in\\mathcal E_e\\).</p><p>**Statement.**\nThe Hom complex between \\(M_P,M_{P'}\\) has a finite equivariant calculation with Euler terms\n\\[\n\\left[\n\\mathbb C[\\ker(e:V\\to V)]\n\\otimes\\operatorname{Hom}_{D_e}(P,P')\n\\otimes\\Lambda^\\bullet(N_e[-1])\n\\otimes\\Lambda^\\bullet(\\operatorname{Lie}(U_e)^*[-1])\n\\right]^{L_e}.\n\\]\nThe coefficient factor has lifted degree zero. The shifts add one to the normal and Lie-factor degrees in Proposition 4. This is an equality of alternating equivariant characters and a finite-filtration weight calculation, not a splitting of the Hom complex.</p><p>For pure coefficient normalizations, all nonconstant terms have strictly negative absolute \\(S_e\\)-weight, and\n\\[\nH^\\bullet\\operatorname{Hom}(M_P,M_{P'})_{\\mathrm{wt}=0}\n=\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{D_e,L_e}(P,P')[0]\n\\]\nwith ordinary composition.</p><p>Before the invariant calculation, weight pieces are finite-dimensional and grow at most polynomially over bounded-length weight intervals. The character sums converge absolutely, uniformly on every normalized compact \\(L_e\\)-coset.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take an \\(L_e\\)-stable lowest-weight slice transverse to \\(J.e\\) in the \\(z\\)-space, with tangent \\(N_e\\). Moving it by \\(J\\) is smooth near \\(e\\), so it computes the equivariant transverse Hom fiber. Then take \\(U_e\\)-cochains and exact \\(L_e\\)-invariants.</p><p>The second input is pushed forward from \\(n=0\\) in the slice and \\(x_{&lt;-1}=0\\). Stabilize the first input by tensoring its Clifford differential with Koszul factorizations of these equations and their potential partners. The difference of the full potential from \\(Q_e(x_{-1})\\) is a sum of equation times partner: \\(n\\)-terms have quadratic \\(x\\)-partners, and \\(x_i\\), \\(i&lt;-1\\), has partner of grade \\(-i-2\\ge0\\). The correction makes the differential square to the full potential. Hom into the second pushforward sets normal equations to zero, leaving\n\\[\n\\Lambda(N_e[-1]).\n\\]</p><p>Filter finitely by these exterior generators. The remaining \\(x\\)-Koszul differential pairs grade \\(i&lt;-1\\) with grade \\(-i-2\\). On each irreducible \\(\\mathfrak{sl}_2\\)-summand the pairing is nondegenerate except at its highest-weight vector, so cohomology is \\(\\mathbb C[\\ker e]\\). Terms involving \\(n\\) may create further differentials, but do not change Euler terms.</p><p>For the nondegenerate quadratic part, the differential on\n\\[\n\\mathbb C[V_{-1}]\\otimes\\operatorname{Hom}(P,P')\n\\]\nis the coordinate sum of Clifford supercommutators. For an orthonormal Clifford basis and homogeneous coefficient map \\(a\\), put\n\\[\nL_i(a)=c_i'a,\\qquad\nR_i(a)=(-1)^{|a|}ac_i,\\qquad\nT_i=L_i-R_i,\\qquad\nK_i=\\tfrac14(L_i+R_i).\n\\]\nClifford anticommutation gives\n\\[\n\\{T_i,T_j\\}=\\{K_i,K_j\\}=0,\\qquad\n\\{T_i,K_j\\}=\\delta_{ij}.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\sum_i x_iT_i\\) is the polynomial Koszul calculation: its cohomology is the constant common kernel of the \\(T_i\\), the Clifford super-intertwiners, in lifted degree zero. Positive polynomial degrees are acyclic. This works in even and odd dimensions.</p><p>A map \\(a\\) of original parity \\(p\\) is a super-intertwiner precisely when\n\\[\nac(v)=(-1)^pc'(v)a.\n\\]\nThe map \\(a\\mapsto a\\gamma_P^p\\) identifies its vector space with ordinary Clifford intertwiners and respects \\(L_e\\) and triple-preserving cyclic action, since \\(\\lambda(-1)\\) is central in \\(L_e\\) and fixed by that action. Before invariants this is only a representation-space identification. An invariant map commutes with \\(\\gamma_P,\\gamma_{P'}\\), so an invariant super-intertwiner has even parity. After invariants the identification has ordinary composition and gives \\(\\operatorname{Hom}_{D_e,L_e}(P,P')\\).</p><p>The finite Chevalley–Eilenberg complex adds\n\\[\n\\Lambda(\\operatorname{Lie}(U_e)^*[-1]),\n\\]\nproving the Euler formula.</p><p>Functions on \\(\\ker e\\) have weights \\(-1-i\\le-1\\), since its \\(h\\)-weights satisfy \\(i\\ge0\\). Normal factors have weights \\(-2+i\\le-2\\), since \\(N_e\\) consists of lowest-weight vectors with \\(i\\le0\\). Nontrivial Lie-cochain factors have weight \\(-i&lt;0\\). Pure coefficient Hom has weight zero. Thus only the invariant constant coefficient contributes weight zero, in degree zero; no differential enters or leaves it.</p><p>There are finitely many polynomial generators of strictly negative weight and finitely many exterior and coefficient factors. Monomial counts have polynomial growth, while eigenvalue moduli decay geometrically. This proves summability, uniformly under compact twists by Proposition 3. Unipotent parts may remain: traces depend on eigenvalues with multiplicity, not on unitarity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Invariant support on traces and actual supported lifts — original Lemma 5.1.** **Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.** Use the complex component model and curved categories …", "statement": "<p>**Invariant support on traces and actual supported lifts — original Lemma 5.1.**</p><p>**Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.**\nUse the complex component model and curved categories \\(\\mathcal B_\\Omega,\\mathcal B_{\\Omega,0}\\) of Proposition 4. Let \\(\\Omega\\) be a Frobenius-stable conical \\(z\\)-support bound.</p><p>**Statement.**\nInclusion induces an isomorphism\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi,\\operatorname{Ind}\\mathcal B_{\\Omega,0})\n\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\n\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi,\\operatorname{Ind}\\mathcal B_\\Omega).\n\\]\nEvery compact Weil object on the right has a compact Weil lift on the left whose trace class maps to a nonzero scalar multiple of its class.</p><p>Both statements hold for compact opposite categories. In Hom pairings of two such lifts, the finite Koszul construction multiplies each orbit contribution by a constant nonzero factor independent of the compact stabilizer variable.</p>", "proof": "<p>The ideal \\(I\\subset R^J\\) of the invariant origin is generated by a finite Frobenius-stable homogeneous space \\(U\\) of positive-degree invariant polynomials: choose homogeneous algebra generators and take their Frobenius spans in the finitely many polynomial-degree spaces involved. By Proposition 3, inverse Hom transport has strictly negative weights on \\(U\\), and forward transport strictly positive weights. In either convention the coefficient transport \\(F_U\\) has no eigenvalue one, so \\(1-F_U\\) is invertible, including with Jordan blocks.</p><p>For a central scalar \\(a\\), its ordinary and Frobenius-transported actions on twisted Hochschild chains are homotopic by moving \\(a\\) around the bar. Apply this to a basis of \\(U\\), then multiply by \\((1-F_U)^{-1}\\). Every generator of \\(I\\) acts trivially on trace cohomology. Viewed over \\(\\operatorname{Sym}U\\to R^J\\), that cohomology is supported at the origin and is \\(I\\)-torsion. This concerns the ordinary complex, not its completion.</p><p>Let \\(i\\) be the supported inclusion and \\(\\Gamma_I\\) its continuous colocalization. On the ambient category, \\(i\\Gamma_I\\) is tensoring by \\(R\\Gamma_I(R)\\), and \\(i\\) preserves compacts. Cyclicity of trace and continuity in the coefficient bimodule identify the inclusion map with\n\\[\nR\\Gamma_I\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi,\\operatorname{Ind}\\mathcal B_\\Omega)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi,\\operatorname{Ind}\\mathcal B_\\Omega).\n\\]\nFinite Koszul complexes on powers of the equations and their filtered union compute this equality directly. It is an isomorphism because the target cohomology is \\(I\\)-torsion; no completed Hochschild bar is substituted.</p><p>For an actual compact Weil object \\(M\\), let \\(K(U)\\) be the finite equivariant Koszul complex on \\(U\\otimes R\\to R\\). Then \\(M\\otimes K(U)\\) is compact, has invariant-origin support, retains its \\(z\\)-support and induced Weil structure, and satisfies\n\\[\n[M\\otimes K(U)]\n=\n\\left(\\sum_j(-1)^j\\operatorname{Tr}(F_U;\\Lambda^jU)\\right)[M]\n=\n\\det(1-F_U;U)[M].\n\\]\nThe determinant is nonzero. Inverse structures replace \\(F_U\\) by its inverse, still with no eigenvalue one. Rescaling in the trace vector space supplies any desired class while keeping an actual representative.</p><p>In a Hom between two Koszul lifts, the exterior filtration has coefficient terms\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}(\\Lambda^aU,\\Lambda^bU),\n\\]\nwith dual transport on the contravariant side and original transport on the covariant side. Their alternating character is the product of two nonzero determinants. Since the equations are \\(J\\)-invariant, these are constant on every compact \\(L_e\\)-variable. Taking sections supported on an orbit commutes with the finite filtration, so the same factor multiplies each orbit contribution, not only their sum. The argument also proves the opposite-category assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Concentration, injection, and Weil spanning — original Theorem 5.4.** **Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.** Use the global setting of Theorem 1.", "statement": "<p>**Concentration, injection, and Weil spanning — original Theorem 5.4.**</p><p>**Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.**\nUse the global setting of Theorem 1. On a retained fixed component use the quadratic and curved models of Propositions 2–7. Closed invariant nilpotent support bounds are denoted \\(\\Omega\\).</p><p>**Statement.**\nOn each fixed retained component, the trace of every closed invariant nilpotent support category is concentrated in degree zero. For \\(\\Omega'\\subset\\Omega\\),\n\\[\nH^0\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi,\\operatorname{Ind}\\mathcal B_{\\Omega',0})\n\\longrightarrow\nH^0\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi,\\operatorname{Ind}\\mathcal B_{\\Omega,0})\n\\]\nis injective. A finite closed orbit filtration has the orbit traces as its actual successive quotients.</p><p>Every trace class is a finite linear combination of classes of compact objects with invertible Weil structures, chosen in the prescribed support bound. The same holds on the compact opposite side.</p><p>After assembling retained components, these assertions hold for \\(\\mathcal C_Y\\) and \\(\\mathcal C\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal F_Y\\mathcal A_E\\) is the injective image of the supported trace. Every function in \\(\\mathcal A_E\\), and every vector in \\(\\mathcal F_Y\\mathcal A_E\\), is a finite linear combination of corresponding actual compact Weil trace functions. Through nonstandard duality, the opposite assertion supplies all function tests.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a finite filtration of the nilpotent support by closed invariant unions of \\(J\\)-orbits, grouping Frobenius permutations into stable strata. There are finitely many orbits, and Frobenius preserves closure order.</p><p>At each step, Lemma 4.2 gives a compactly generated Frobenius-compatible localization. Twisted Hochschild homology is localizing, giving a cofiber sequence of traces by [HSS, Theorem 3.4 and Proposition 5.4]. Disjoint orbits closed in the open have orthogonal supported categories. A nontrivially permuted collection has zero twisted trace: a nonzero bar chain must begin and end in the same summand, whereas its graph coefficient ends in the Frobenius translate. Each fixed orbit has the degree-zero trace of Lemma 8.2.</p><p>Induction proves concentration. The cofiber sequences become short exact sequences on \\(H^0\\). Applying the same finite orbit argument to the complement of \\(\\Omega'\\) in \\(\\Omega\\) gives\n\\[\n0\\to\nH^0\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi,\\mathcal B_{\\Omega',0})\n\\to\nH^0\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi,\\mathcal B_{\\Omega,0})\n\\to\nH^0\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi,\\mathcal B_{\\Omega,0}/\\mathcal B_{\\Omega',0})\n\\to0,\n\\]\nwith ind-completions understood. This proves injection and identifies actual successive quotients, rather than inferring injection merely from ambient concentration.</p><p>At each step, quotient basis classes lift by Proposition 5 and the finite Koszul construction of Proposition 7. Subtract finitely many such lifts from a class; the remainder belongs to the preceding trace. Finite induction proves spanning with prescribed support. The opposite argument is identical using Lemma 8.2.</p><p>Finally, retained components form a categorical coproduct, with no arrows between distinct components. A twisted bar string closes only in a component fixed by \\(\\Phi\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi,\\mathcal C)\n\\simeq\n\\bigoplus_{\\substack{Z_a\\subset Z\\ \\mathrm{retained}\\\\\\Phi Z_a=Z_a}}\n\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi,\\mathcal C|_{Z_a}),\n\\]\nand similarly for supported categories and compact opposites. A nontrivially permuted component contributes zero even if a power fixes it. Compact objects and trace vectors involve finitely many components.</p><p>Proposition 1 transports these results to automorphic categories and takes each actual compact Weil class to its ordinary finitely supported trace function. This identifies the supported injection with the image defining \\(\\mathcal F_Y\\). Simultaneous-Frobenius nonstandard duality identifies the opposite trace with the full function-test space. The calculations made after an arbitrary \\(E\\simeq\\mathbb C\\) transport back to \\(E\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Locally finite twisted-bar contraction — original Lemma 5.2.** Let a small generating differential graded category with autoequivalence be modeled in complexes of locally finite cyclic representations.", "statement": "<p>**Locally finite twisted-bar contraction — original Lemma 5.2.**</p><p>Let a small generating differential graded category with autoequivalence be modeled in complexes of locally finite cyclic representations. After finite cyclic permutations of labels and pure choices of structures, suppose:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Morphism cohomology has nonpositive absolute \\(S\\)-weights.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Composition adds weights.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Weight-zero morphism cohomology is concentrated in degree zero.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Its additive idempotent completion is split semisimple, and each simple has endomorphism algebra \\(\\mathbb C\\).</p><p>Then its twisted trace is the twisted trace of that split semisimple category. It is concentrated in degree zero, with one basis line for each fixed simple label. Nontrivially permuted simple labels contribute zero. The trace computed is that of the original autoequivalence, not its inverse or a power.</p>", "proof": "<p>On a finite stable list of models, let \\(\\pi\\) be the label permutation and choose\n\\[\n\\alpha_i:\\Phi M_i\\xrightarrow{\\sim}M_{\\pi i}.\n\\]\nThey give a transported functor \\(\\Psi\\), with\n\\[\nT(a)=\\Psi(a)=\\alpha_j\\Phi(a)\\alpha_i^{-1},\n\\qquad\nS(b)=\\Phi^{-1}(\\alpha_j^{-1}b\\alpha_i).\n\\]\nFor the left-twisted graph\n\\[\nG_\\Phi(i,j)=\\operatorname{Hom}(M_i,\\Phi M_j),\n\\]\nthe maps\n\\[\n\\theta_{ij}:G_\\Phi(i,j)\\longrightarrow\nG_\\Psi(i,j)=\\operatorname{Hom}(M_i,M_{\\pi j}),\n\\qquad m\\longmapsto\\alpha_jm,\n\\]\nidentify graph bimodules:\n\\[\n\\theta(\\Phi(a)mb)=\\Psi(a)\\theta(m)b.\n\\]\nThe original raw graph operator is\n\\[\nT_G(m)=\\Phi(\\alpha_j)\\Phi(m)\\alpha_i^{-1},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\theta_{\\pi i,\\pi j}(T_G(m))=\\Psi(\\theta_{ij}(m)).\n\\]\nFor one algebra, \\(\\psi=\\operatorname{Ad}_\\alpha\\varphi\\), these formulas are\n\\[\n\\theta(m)=\\alpha m,\\qquad\nT_G(m)=\\varphi(\\alpha)\\varphi(m)\\alpha^{-1}.\n\\]\nTransport the entire bar construction through this graph identification.</p><p>Take nonpositive-weight subcomplexes in each morphism complex. They form a differential graded subcategory and are quasi-equivalent to the original one, because excluded weight pieces have zero cohomology. Locally finite semisimple-weight decomposition is exact, also for unbounded complexes.</p><p>Use the direct-sum twisted cyclic bar. Each chain lies in finitely many finite-dimensional cyclic subrepresentations. On a finite permutation-stable list, a common power fixes labels and reads the same divided absolute weights; only the weights use the power, not the functor or graph.</p><p>In \\(G_\\Psi\\)-coordinates, cyclic rotation has one-algebra formula\n\\[\nt_n(a_0|\\cdots|a_n)\n=\n(\\Psi(a_n)|a_0|\\cdots|a_{n-1})\n\\]\nwith Koszul signs. Its \\((n+1)\\)-fold iterate applies \\(\\Psi\\) to all factors. Inserting the unit and summing rotations gives the paracyclic identity\n\\[\ndH+Hd=1-T.\n\\]\nConsecutive faces cancel, leaving the identity and full rotation. Transport gives the identity on the original graph. In degree zero, if\n\\[\nd(m|a)=ma-\\varphi(a)m,\n\\]\nthen \\(H_0(m)=\\alpha^{-1}|\\alpha m\\) gives \\(dH_0(m)=m-T_G(m)\\).</p><p>Since \\(T\\) commutes with \\(d\\),\n\\[\nH_{\\mathrm{inv}}=-T^{-1}H,\\qquad\ndH_{\\mathrm{inv}}+H_{\\mathrm{inv}}d=1-S.\n\\]\nOn strictly negative-weight blocks, \\(1-S\\) is invertible algebraically: on each finite-dimensional cyclic subrepresentation its inverse is a polynomial in \\(S\\), and these inverses agree on inclusions. Therefore\n\\[\n(1-S)^{-1}H_{\\mathrm{inv}}\n\\]\ncontracts the block. There is no infinite geometric series of chains. A power used to read weights gives the same conclusion because eigenvalues of \\(S\\) still have modulus less than one.</p><p>A weight-zero bar term contains only weight-zero arrows, so the remaining bar is the weight-zero category’s bar. Canonical differential graded truncation gives a cyclic-compatible zigzag to its degree-zero cohomology category. Morita reduction to its simple objects leaves only scalar endomorphisms. A fixed simple gives \\(\\mathbb C[0]\\); a permuted block gives zero.</p><p>Every chain uses finitely many labels, enlarged to a finite stable list. Filtered union proves the general case. All realizations are direct sums; neither infinite products nor convergence of an infinite Hochschild spectral sequence is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Trace of an orbit — original Proposition 5.3.** Use the fixed-component setting of Proposition 8, and let \\(O=J.e\\) be a Frobenius-stable nilpotent orbit.", "statement": "<p>**Trace of an orbit — original Proposition 5.3.**</p><p>Use the fixed-component setting of Proposition 8, and let \\(O=J.e\\) be a Frobenius-stable nilpotent orbit.</p><p>Its supported trace is concentrated in degree zero, with basis the classes of \\(M_P\\) for Frobenius-fixed simple \\(P\\in\\mathcal E_e\\), with chosen pure normalized structures. Every basis class lifts to an actual compact Weil object on \\(\\overline O\\), and, up to nonzero scalar, to one with invariant null-fiber support.</p><p>The same holds for compact opposite categories with simultaneous Frobenius.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 4, simple-label objects generate, so Morita invariance permits computing the trace on their full subcategory.</p><p>To verify local finiteness on complexes, keep grading internal until direct-sum totalization. For a finite cyclic label list, choose a common finite-type algebraic cover \\(H\\) of the equivariance and cyclic chain-map data, acting on the objects and an invariant quasi-compact separated ambient open \\(U\\). Polynomial functions, finite Koszul resolutions, restriction, and proper pushforward are defined in rational \\(H\\)-representations.</p><p>Compute sections without a transporter-stable affine cover: equivariant pushforward along\n\\[\n[U/H]\\to BH\n\\]\nproduces complexes of rational representations. Flat base change by \\(\\operatorname{pt}\\to BH\\) recovers ordinary sections, and lax monoidal pushforward supplies composition [DG, §1.3.10, Corollary 1.4.5, §§6.4.3–6.4.6]. Differential Hom is square-zero because curvatures cancel. Functorial bar–cobar rectification uses direct sums of finite tensor words, preserves rationality and cyclic action, and gives strict differential graded composition [Kel, Proposition 2.1 and §§4.3, 4.8, 5.2].</p><p>Restriction to the algebraic closure of the cyclic operator makes the action locally finite. Stabilizer invariants are the finite Lie-cochain complex followed by exact reductive invariants, commuting with direct-sum folding. Character decomposition of the semisimple part gives the weight subcomplexes of Lemma 8.1.</p><p>Proposition 5 supplies finite cyclic label orbits and consistent pure implementing maps. Triple translation by a group element does not change the final invariant arrow action \\(S\\). Proposition 6 gives nonpositive-weight cohomology and identifies weight zero with the semisimple coefficient category, whose simples have scalar endomorphisms by Schur’s Lemma. Lemma 8.1 proves concentration and the basis. Proposition 5 gives actual orbit-closure representatives, and Proposition 7 gives invariant-supported representatives.</p><p>For the opposite category, use the same objects with reversed arrows and inverse structures. Simultaneous forward action on a reversed arrow is forward action on the underlying original arrow; its inverse is the same contracting \\(S\\). The weights and paracyclic identity are unchanged. This uses \\(\\Phi^{\\mathrm{op}}\\), not the inverse autoequivalence of the original category, and gives actual inverse Weil representatives.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Compact spectral sets and uniqueness of measures — original Lemma 7.1 and Corollary 7.2.** **Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.** Let \\(\\widehat G_{\\mathbb C}\\) be the fixed complex split dual group.", "statement": "<p>**Compact spectral sets and uniqueness of measures — original Lemma 7.1 and Corollary 7.2.**</p><p>**Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.**\nLet \\(\\widehat G_{\\mathbb C}\\) be the fixed complex split dual group. Fix \\(r=q^d&gt;1\\). For each nilpotent orbit \\(o\\subset\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}_{\\mathbb C}\\), choose a triple with nilpotent \\(e\\in o\\), associated homomorphism \\(\\operatorname{SL}_{2,e}\\to\\widehat G_{\\mathbb C}\\), and diagonal cocharacter \\(\\lambda_e\\), with \\(e\\) of weight two. Define\n\\[\nB_o=\n\\left\\{\n[\\lambda_e(\\sqrt r)s]:\ns\\in Z_{\\widehat G_{\\mathbb C}}(\\operatorname{SL}_{2,e})_{\\mathrm{cpt}}\n\\right\\}\n\\subset\\widehat G_{\\mathbb C}/\\!/\\widehat G_{\\mathbb C},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\sqrt r&gt;0\\), brackets mean semisimple conjugacy value, and the compact form includes all components. Put \\(B=\\bigcup_oB_o\\).</p><p>**Statement.**\nThere are finitely many \\(B_o\\). Each is compact and independent of triple and compact-form choices; different nilpotent orbits give disjoint sets.</p><p>The algebra of representation characters restricted to \\(B\\) contains constants, separates points, is closed under complex conjugation, and is uniformly dense in \\(C(B,\\mathbb C)\\). Each \\(B_o\\) is preserved by inversion and multiplication by finite-order central elements.</p><p>Consequently a finite complex Borel measure on \\(B\\) is determined by its integrals against all representation characters.</p>", "proof": "<p>Nilpotent orbits in a complex semisimple Lie algebra are finite in number. Triples within an orbit are conjugate, as are maximal compact forms of a complex reductive group including components. Their images in the conjugacy quotient are therefore independent of choices and compact. The finite union \\(B\\) is a compact metrizable subspace of a complex affine variety.</p><p>For a maximal torus \\(T\\),\n\\[\nT(\\mathbb C)=T(\\mathbb R_{&gt;0})\\times T_{\\mathrm{cpt}},\n\\]\nwith positive part intrinsically determined by absolute values of all characters. This decomposition is Weyl equivariant. The centralizer of \\(\\lambda_e(\\mathbb G_m)\\) is a connected reductive Levi containing \\(s\\). A maximal torus of that Levi containing \\(s\\) also contains its central torus \\(\\lambda_e(\\mathbb G_m)\\). Thus \\(\\lambda_e,s\\) lie in a common maximal torus, and the positive part of \\(\\lambda_e(\\sqrt r)s\\) is exactly \\(\\lambda_e(\\sqrt r)\\). Its Weyl orbit determines the dominant cocharacter, since evaluation at \\(\\sqrt r&gt;1\\) is injective on cocharacters.</p><p>This cocharacter determines the nilpotent orbit, retaining type \\(D\\) distinctions. Fix \\(\\lambda\\), and let \\(\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}_i\\) be its weight spaces. For a triple with diagonal cocharacter \\(\\lambda\\),\n\\[\n[\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}_0,e]=\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}_2,\n\\]\nbecause raising from weight zero to two is surjective on each irreducible \\(\\mathfrak{sl}_2\\)-summand. The centralizer of \\(\\lambda\\) therefore has an open orbit through \\(e\\) in the irreducible space \\(\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}_2\\); two such open orbits coincide. Equal positive parts imply equal ambient nilpotent orbits, proving disjointness, as in [GLR, Lemma 3.1.3].</p><p>Representation characters separate semisimple conjugacy values: their complex span restricts to \\(\\mathbb C[T]^W\\) [Mil, Theorem 22.38]. Write \\(a=\\lambda_e(\\sqrt r)\\). In a representation \\(D\\), \\(D(a)\\) has positive real eigenvalues and commutes with \\(D(s)\\), which is unitary on its eigenspaces after averaging. Hence\n\\[\n\\overline{\\chi_D(as)}=\\chi_D(as^{-1}).\n\\]\nA Weyl element in the triple’s \\(\\operatorname{SL}_2\\) conjugates \\(a\\) to \\(a^{-1}\\) and commutes with \\(s\\), so\n\\[\n\\chi_D(as^{-1})\n=\n\\chi_D(a^{-1}s^{-1})\n=\n\\chi_{D^\\vee}(as).\n\\]\nThe same dual representation works on all \\(B_o\\). The algebra is self-adjoint, and complex Stone–Weierstrass gives density.</p><p>The same triple Weyl element shows \\((as)^{-1}\\) conjugate to \\(as^{-1}\\), proving inversion invariance. A finite-order central element lies in the chosen compact triple centralizer: adjoining it gives a compact group, unchanged by maximality. Thus its multiplication preserves \\(B_o\\).</p><p>Finally, integration against a finite complex measure is a continuous functional for the uniform norm. Equality on characters gives equality on \\(C(B,\\mathbb C)\\), hence equality of measures.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Hecke moment measures and their support bound — the existence and uniqueness part of original Theorem 6.1 and original Proposition 6.4.** **Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.** Use the global setting of Theorem …", "statement": "<p>**Hecke moment measures and their support bound — the existence and uniqueness part of original Theorem 6.1 and original Proposition 6.4.**</p><p>**Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.**\nUse the global setting of Theorem 1, fix an abstract \\(E\\simeq\\mathbb C\\), and fix a closed point \\(v\\) of degree \\(d\\), with \\(r=q^d\\). Use \\(B_o,B\\) from Proposition 9.</p><p>For a finite-dimensional representation \\(D\\) of \\(\\widehat G_{\\mathbb C}\\), let \\(T_D\\) be the spherical Hecke operator at \\(v\\) in positive square-root Satake normalization. On \\(\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb C}\\), use the bilinear pairing\n\\[\n[f,g]=\\sum_{b\\in S}\\frac{f(b)g(b)}{|\\operatorname{Aut}(b)|}.\n\\]\nA finite complex measure means one of finite total variation.</p><p>**Statement.**\nFor every \\(f,g\\in\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb C}\\), there is a unique finite complex Borel measure \\(\\nu_{f,g}\\) on \\(B\\) such that\n\\[\n[T_Df,g]=\\int_B\\chi_D\\,d\\nu_{f,g}\n\\]\nfor every \\(D\\). The construction is bilinear.</p><p>More specifically, let \\(M,N\\) be compact spectral Weil objects on one retained fixed component, with closed nilpotent \\(z\\)-support bounds \\(Z_M,Z_N\\subset\\mathfrak j\\). Let \\(f_N\\) be the covariant function and \\(g_M\\) its opposite-side function test. Then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{supp}(\\nu_{f_N,g_M})\n\\subset\n\\bigcup_{e\\in Z_M\\cap Z_N}B_{\\widehat G.e}.\n\\]\nConsequently, for every closed invariant \\(Y\\subset\\widehat{\\mathcal N}\\),\n\\[\nf\\in\\mathcal F_Y\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb C}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\operatorname{supp}(\\nu_{f,g})\\subset B_Y\n\\quad\\text{for all }g,\n\\qquad\nB_Y=\\bigcup_{o\\subset Y_{\\mathbb C}}B_o.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For a stable orbit \\(O=J.e\\), let \\(L_{e,c}\\) be the maximal compact of Proposition 3, including components. Let \\(\\tau_e\\) denote the triple-adjusted inverse action, retaining the absolute-weight scalars. Write functions on its compact transporter coset as functions of \\(k\\in L_{e,c}\\), with operator \\(k\\tau_e\\), and let \\(dk\\) be probability Haar measure on the entire compact group.</p><p>Reynolds projection gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{str}(\\tau_e;H^{L_e})\n=\n\\int_{L_{e,c}}\\operatorname{str}(k\\tau_e;H)\\,dk.\n\\]\nThe projector commutes with \\(\\tau_e\\), which normalizes the compact group. Lemma 10.2 extends the finite-dimensional formula by uniform absolute convergence, including disconnected groups.</p><p>For linear maps around \\(d\\) copies of \\(D\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}\\!\\left(\n\\operatorname{cyc}\\circ(A_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes A_d);D^{\\otimes d}\n\\right)\n=\n\\operatorname{tr}(A_d\\cdots A_1;D).\n\\]\nExpansion in a basis proves this cyclic trace identity. Proposition 2’s framed description applies it to evaluation above \\(v\\). Triple translations contribute \\(\\lambda_e(q^{d/2})\\); the residual semisimple product is compact and centralizes the triple by Proposition 3. Unipotent factors may be discarded in this trace. Thus there is a continuous map\n\\[\n\\beta_e:L_{e,c}\\longrightarrow B_{\\widehat G.e}.\n\\]\nA lifted-degree sign on insertion contributes \\(\\lambda_e(-1)\\) to the compact part, already included in this set. Contravariant character conventions give inversion, preserving the set by Proposition 9.</p><p>Remove insertion and denote the supported alternating character density by \\(a_{M,N,e}(k)\\). Lifted-degree signs compute true alternating signs after invariants, by Proposition 6. Lemma 10.2 makes this density continuous and integrable. The orbit contributes the finite complex measure\n\\[\n(\\beta_e)_*\\bigl(a_{M,N,e}(k)\\,dk\\bigr),\n\\]\nwhose variation is at most \\(\\int|a_{M,N,e}|\\,dk\\). Its \\(\\chi_D\\)-moment is precisely the orbit’s inserted contribution.</p><p>Sum over stable nilpotent orbits in the common support. Outside that support, differential Hom is zero; permuted orbits contribute zero. Lemmas 10.1–10.2 give the required moments and support bound. Proposition 9 proves uniqueness.</p><p>By Proposition 8, every pair \\(f,g\\) is a finite linear combination of such pairs, using finitely many fixed retained components. Cross-component Homs vanish. Adding finitely many measures proves existence for all functions. Character-density uniqueness makes the result independent of expansions and proves bilinearity. Taking \\(N\\) in prescribed \\(Y\\)-support proves the final implication.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Bilinear Hom formula — original Lemma 6.2.** Use the setting of Proposition 10.", "statement": "<p>**Bilinear Hom formula — original Lemma 6.2.**</p><p>Use the setting of Proposition 10. Let \\(N,M\\) be compact spectral objects with structures\n\\[\nb:N\\to\\Phi N,\\qquad a:\\Phi M\\to M.\n\\]\nTransport \\(M\\) through categorical duality to its automorphic test function \\(g_M\\), and let \\(f_N\\) be the function of \\(N\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal E_{D,v}\\) denote evaluation insertion at the cyclic tuple above \\(v\\), with its cyclic Weil transport. Then\n\\[\n[T_Df_N,g_M]\n=\n\\operatorname{str}\\!\\left(\nS_{M,N,D};\nR\\operatorname{Hom}(M,\\mathcal E_{D,v}\\otimes N)\n\\right),\n\\]\nwhere without insertion\n\\[\nS_{M,N}(h)=\\Phi^{-1}(b\\circ h\\circ a).\n\\]\nThe scalar evaluation complex is perfect.</p><p>Finite linear combinations of these classes give every input and every test in \\(\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb C}\\). Inputs in \\(\\mathcal F_Y\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb C}\\) can be represented by \\(N\\) with prescribed \\(Y\\)-support.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 1 gives compact embedding into ambient sheaves, preservation of compactness by star tensor product on compact inputs, and preservation by finite Hecke insertion. Compact pushforward to a point preserves compactness, whose meaning in vector spaces is perfection. These finiteness assertions are needed in addition to abstract self-duality.</p><p>The ordinary local-term comparison and sheaf–function dictionary identify the evaluation trace with\n\\[\n\\sum_{b\\in\\operatorname{Bun}_G(\\mathbb F_q)/\\simeq}\n\\frac{(T_Df_N)(b)g_M(b)}{|\\operatorname{Aut}(b)|}.\n\\]\nFor a fixed Hecke label, the correspondences are bounded and compact inputs have quasi-compact !-support, so the compact local-term theorem applies to this evaluation. Transport through duality gives the indicated inverse Hom action and formula.</p><p>Proposition 8 supplies actual compact Weil-class spanning on both covariant and opposite sides, including every supported image. The trace–functions isomorphism proves the final assertions. Duality need not preserve spectral components or support labels: choose \\(M\\) on the Hom side, then obtain its function test. Distinct components have zero Hom.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Supported convergence — original Lemma 6.3.** Fix a retained Frobenius-stable component with curved model \\(W(x,z)=\\langle z,\\mu(x)\\rangle\\).", "statement": "<p>**Supported convergence — original Lemma 6.3.**</p><p>Fix a retained Frobenius-stable component with curved model \\(W(x,z)=\\langle z,\\mu(x)\\rangle\\). For compact lifts \\(M,N\\), let\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\mathcal Hom(M,N)\n\\]\nbe differential Hom computed with locally free representatives; its differential squares to zero. Its derived \\(J\\)-invariant sections, with evaluation insertion, compute Lemma 10.1.</p><p>Let \\(O=J.e\\) be a stable nilpotent orbit closed in an invariant open subset of \\(\\mathfrak j\\). The contribution with support on \\(O\\) has an absolutely convergent alternating character on the compact transporter coset of \\(L_e\\), uniformly there and after any fixed finite-dimensional evaluation insertion.</p><p>These characters are additive through the finite orbit filtration and compute the ordinary supertrace of the final perfect evaluation complex.</p>", "proof": "<p>The support of \\(\\mathcal H\\) lies in the intersection of factorization supports, because where either object vanishes locally, differential Hom is acyclic. Filter sections by local cohomology with supports in nilpotent \\(z\\)-orbits, not ordinary restriction.</p><p>Let \\(i:O\\hookrightarrow U\\subset\\mathfrak j\\), with ideal \\(I\\) and codimension \\(c\\). It is a regular immersion, whose normal bundle has fiber\n\\[\nN_e=\\mathfrak j/[\\mathfrak j,e].\n\\]\nLocal cohomology is\n\\[\nR\\Gamma_O(\\mathcal H)\n=\n\\operatorname*{colim}_n\nR\\mathcal Hom(\\mathcal O_U/I^{n+1},\\mathcal H).\n\\]\nIts successive derived cofibers are\n\\[\ni_*\\!\\left(\nLi^*\\mathcal H\\otimes\\operatorname{Sym}^nN\\otimes\\det N[-c]\n\\right),\n\\qquad n\\ge0.\n\\]\nIndeed,\n\\[\nI^n/I^{n+1}=\\operatorname{Sym}^nN^*,\n\\qquad\ni^!\\mathcal H=Li^*\\mathcal H\\otimes\\det N[-c].\n\\]\nBounded regular Koszul resolutions justify the formula before direct-sum folding. The determinant is the normal determinant.</p><p>For a degree-two coordinate with \\(S(z)=qz\\),\n\\[\nH^1_{(z)}\\mathbb C[z]\n=\n\\bigoplus_{m\\ge1}\\mathbb Cz^{-m},\n\\qquad\nS(z^{-m})=q^{-m}z^{-m}.\n\\]\nThe total degree is \\(1-2m\\), checking both sign and shift.</p><p>Take the fiber at \\(e\\), then derived \\(C_e=L_e\\ltimes U_e\\)-invariants. The restricted Hom cohomology is coherent over \\(\\mathbb C[V]\\), supported in \\(\\ker e\\). Differentiate the target factorization:\n\\[\nd_N(\\partial d_N)+(\\partial d_N)d_N=\\partial W.\n\\]\nLeft composition with \\(\\partial d_N\\) is a homotopy for \\(\\partial W\\) on differential Hom. At \\(z=e\\), \\(V\\)-derivatives give the equations \\(ex=0\\).</p><p>Coherence makes a power of the ideal of \\(\\ker e\\) annihilate the cohomology. Hence only finitely many transverse \\(x\\)-orders occur, while normal orders remain unbounded. After triple translation,\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{space}&amp;\\text{absolute }S\\text{-weight}\\\\ \\hline\n(\\ker e\\cap V_i)^*&amp;-1-i\\le-1,\\quad i\\ge0\\\\\n(N_e)_i&amp;-2+i\\le-2,\\quad i\\le0\\\\\n\\operatorname{Lie}(U_e)_i^*&amp;-i&lt;0,\\quad i&gt;0.\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThus unrestricted \\(x\\)-variables and normal symmetric powers contract. Transverse thickenings, determinant, and finite unipotent cochains contribute only finite coefficients.</p><p>The locally finite algebraic models in Lemma 8.2 permit a finite-dimensional invariant generating space for the restricted cohomology and its finite thickening. For constants \\(C,a,b\\) and \\(0&lt;\\rho_x,\\rho_z&lt;1\\), eigenvalue moduli with multiplicities in \\(x\\)-degree \\(m\\) and normal order \\(n\\) are bounded by\n\\[\nC(m+1)^a(n+1)^b\\rho_x^m\\rho_z^n.\n\\]\nOne may take \\(\\rho_x=q^{-1/2}\\), \\(\\rho_z=q^{-1}\\), absorbing finite coefficients into \\(C\\). Polynomial growth follows from finitely many variables; subquotients decrease generalized-weight multiplicities.</p><p>Proposition 3 makes these bounds uniform under compact \\(L_e\\)-twists. The semisimple normalized parts preserve a common compact form, and the residual commuting unipotent does not change eigenvalue moduli. Finite coefficients only change the uniform constant.</p><p>For every \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), only finitely many \\((m,n)\\) have eigenvalues of modulus at least \\(\\epsilon\\), since coefficient weights lie in a finite set. Taking the sum of generalized eigenspaces in this range is exact in locally finite representations. On that range, the filtration stabilizes after finitely many normal orders, so ordinary long exact sequences give additivity. Exactness of filtered colimits identifies the same part of colimit cohomology. The displayed summable bound permits \\(\\epsilon\\downarrow0\\), also through the finite orbit filtration. Bounded Koszul and group-cochain calculations commute with direct-sum folding.</p><p>For a finite permuted set of strata, use a power \\(S^t\\) for estimates. Geometric bounds remain summable after taking the \\(t\\)-th roots of eigenvalue moduli, and one-step \\(S\\) has zero trace on the permuted sum. Fixed strata have the preceding calculation. The final evaluation is perfect by Lemma 10.1, so the convergent alternating character is its ordinary finite supertrace.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Bilinear support detector — original Theorem 6.1.** **Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.** Use the fixed closed point, complex coefficient identification, bilinear pairing, Hecke operators, compact sets, and …", "statement": "<p>**Bilinear support detector — original Theorem 6.1.**</p><p>**Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.**\nUse the fixed closed point, complex coefficient identification, bilinear pairing, Hecke operators, compact sets, and uniquely determined measures \\(\\nu_{f,g}\\) of Proposition 10.</p><p>**Statement.**\nFor every closed invariant \\(Y\\subset\\widehat{\\mathcal N}\\) and every \\(f\\in\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb C}\\),\n\\[\nf\\in\\mathcal F_Y\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb C}\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\operatorname{supp}(\\nu_{f,g})\\subset B_Y\n\\quad\\text{for every }g\\in\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb C}.\n\\]\nTogether with Proposition 10, this gives both assertions of original Theorem 6.1. No positivity of the measures is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>The forward implication is Proposition 10.</p><p>Suppose\n\\[\nf\\notin\\mathcal F_Y\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb C}.\n\\]\nBy Proposition 8, select a fixed retained component on which its class is not in the supported trace for \\(Y\\cap\\mathfrak j\\). Starting with this closed subset, filter the nilpotent cone of \\(\\mathfrak j\\) by closed invariant subsets, adding a Frobenius orbit of \\(J\\)-orbits at each step, compatibly with closure. Let\n\\[\nT_0\\subset T_1\\subset\\cdots\\subset T_m\n\\]\nbe the supported trace subspaces, with \\(T_0\\) the trace for \\(Y\\cap\\mathfrak j\\).</p><p>Choose the least \\(i\\) containing the component class. Then \\(i&gt;0\\), and its image\n\\[\nu\\in T_i/T_{i-1}\n\\]\nis nonzero. A nontrivially permuted stratum has zero trace by Proposition 8. Thus this step adds one stable orbit \\(O=J.e\\) outside \\(Y\\cap\\mathfrak j\\).</p><p>Express \\(u\\) as a finite combination of fixed-simple quotient classes, and choose compact orbit-closure lifts. Subtracting their combination leaves a class in \\(T_{i-1}\\), represented by finitely many compact Weil classes with the earlier support. Lemma 11.2 supplies a compact test lift with nonzero top mass for \\(u\\).</p><p>The earlier support meets \\(\\overline O\\) only in \\(\\overline O\\setminus O\\). Proposition 10 and Lemma 11.3 show that the remainder and proper-boundary contributions have zero mass on \\(B_{\\widehat G.e}\\). This also excludes incomparable \\(J\\)-orbits lying in the same ambient orbit: common support with the test closure lies in its proper boundary. Other retained components have zero Hom. Hence the resulting genuine finitely supported test \\(g\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\nu_{f,g}(B_{\\widehat G.e})\\ne0.\n\\]\nSince \\(e\\notin Y\\), the ambient invariant subset \\(Y\\) does not contain \\(\\widehat G.e\\), and Proposition 9 makes \\(B_{\\widehat G.e}\\) disjoint from \\(B_Y\\). The support condition fails.</p><p>All inputs and tests remain finite linear combinations of compact Weil classes. No rational point, cuspidality, or Hecke-finiteness was used; the closed point may have any positive degree.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Cancellation of the universal density — original Lemma 6.5.** Fix a stable orbit \\(O=J.e\\), coefficients \\(P,Q\\in\\mathcal E_e\\) with compatible cyclic structures, and their compact orbit-closure lifts of Proposition 5.", "statement": "<p>**Cancellation of the universal density — original Lemma 6.5.**</p><p>Fix a stable orbit \\(O=J.e\\), coefficients \\(P,Q\\in\\mathcal E_e\\) with compatible cyclic structures, and their compact orbit-closure lifts of Proposition 5. Put \\(Q\\) in the test variable and \\(P\\) in the input variable.</p><p>On the open where \\(O\\) is closed and the proper boundary of \\(\\overline O\\) has been removed, the top-orbit density is\n\\[\na_{Q,P,e}(k)\n=\np_e(k)\\operatorname{tr}\\!\\left(k\\tau_e;\\operatorname{Hom}_{D_e}(Q,P)\\right),\n\\qquad k\\in L_{e,c},\n\\]\nwhere \\(p_e\\) is the graded Euler character of\n\\[\n\\mathbb C[\\ker e]\\otimes\n\\Lambda^\\bullet(N_e[-1])\\otimes\n\\Lambda^\\bullet(\\operatorname{Lie}(U_e)^*[-1]).\n\\]\nInvariant-base Koszul lifts change this formula only by constant nonzero factors, which may be removed by rescaling trace classes.</p><p>The function \\(p_e\\) is continuous and nowhere zero. Its reciprocal is a uniform limit of finite complex linear combinations of twisted characters of finite-dimensional \\(L_e\\)-representations with compatible transporter structures. Such a character is inserted by tensoring the test coefficient with the dual representation in lifted degree zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>On the specified open, both lifts are supported on \\(O\\), so local cohomology is actual Hom. There is no additional inverse-normal-power series. Proposition 6 gives the displayed density, with the inverse structure in the test variable. Proposition 7 says invariant-base Koszul constructions multiply each orbit contribution by constant nonzero determinants, since equation spaces are \\(J\\)-invariant.</p><p>Decompose the finite-dimensional spaces by lifted degree. Symmetric algebras give inverse determinants, and exterior algebras give determinants. Hence \\(p_e\\) is a finite product\n\\[\n\\det(1-\\varepsilon kA;W)^\\eta,\n\\qquad\n\\varepsilon\\in\\{1,-1\\},\\quad\n\\eta\\in\\{1,-1\\},\n\\]\nwhere all eigenvalues of \\(kA\\) have modulus strictly less than one, uniformly in \\(k\\), by Proposition 6’s strict negative weights and Proposition 3’s compact normalization. The sign incorporates the lifted-degree shift. Every determinant is nonzero.</p><p>Use\n\\[\n\\det(1-A;W)\n=\n\\sum_{j=0}^{\\dim W}(-1)^j\\operatorname{tr}(A;\\Lambda^jW),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\det(1-A;W)^{-1}\n=\n\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\operatorname{tr}(A;\\operatorname{Sym}^nW),\n\\]\nalso with the sign \\(\\varepsilon\\). The latter converges uniformly because its \\(n\\)-th term is bounded by \\(\\dim(\\operatorname{Sym}^nW)\\rho^n\\) for fixed \\(\\rho&lt;1\\). Finite products express \\(1/p_e\\) as the required uniform limit. Terms are twisted characters of finite tensor products of symmetric and exterior powers; scalars may enter coefficients or cyclic structures.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hom}_{D_e}(Q\\otimes R^*,P)\n\\simeq\nR\\otimes\\operatorname{Hom}_{D_e}(Q,P)\n\\]\nwith induced cyclic actions. Proposition 6’s parity identification makes the density multiply by the twisted character of \\(R\\). Proposition 5 extends \\(Q\\otimes R^*\\), so every finite approximation is a finite linear combination of permitted actual compact test classes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Top quotient detection — original Lemma 6.6.** Use the stable orbit and coefficient setting of Lemma 11.1.", "statement": "<p>**Top quotient detection — original Lemma 6.6.**</p><p>Use the stable orbit and coefficient setting of Lemma 11.1. Every nonzero finite linear combination of Frobenius-fixed simple classes in the \\(O\\)-quotient is detected by a permitted compact orbit-closure test lift: its top-orbit measure has nonzero total mass on \\(B_{\\widehat G.e}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write the quotient class as\n\\[\nu=\\sum_Pc_P[P],\n\\]\nwith finitely many nonzero coefficients. Suppose its top mass vanished against every permitted test lift. Fix \\(Q\\). Tensoring \\(Q\\) by duals of the representations in Lemma 11.1 gives\n\\[\n\\int_{L_{e,c}}\np_e(k)h(k)\n\\sum_Pc_P\n\\operatorname{tr}\\!\\left(k\\tau_e;\\operatorname{Hom}_{D_e}(Q,P)\\right)\n\\,dk\n=0\n\\]\nfor every finite character combination \\(h\\) used there.</p><p>Let \\(h\\) converge uniformly to \\(1/p_e\\). This is integration against a fixed continuous function, not a limit of automorphic functions. Reynolds projection gives\n\\[\n0=\n\\sum_Pc_P\n\\operatorname{tr}\\!\\left(\n\\tau_e;\\operatorname{Hom}_{D_e,L_e}(Q,P)\n\\right).\n\\]\nChoose \\(Q\\) to be a fixed simple appearing in \\(u\\), with matching structure. Semisimplicity gives zero Hom for \\(P\\not\\simeq Q\\); for \\(P\\simeq Q\\), Hom is the scalar line with identity action. Thus \\(c_Q=0\\). Repeating contradicts \\(u\\ne0\\), so some actual finite test has nonzero top mass.</p><p>No injectivity of \\(\\beta_e:L_{e,c}\\to B_{\\widehat G.e}\\) is needed: total mass suffices.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Strict ambient rank drop on the boundary — original Lemma 6.7.** Let \\(J\\subset\\widehat G_{\\mathbb C}\\) be the reductive stabilizer of a retained component, with Lie algebra \\(\\mathfrak j\\).", "statement": "<p>**Strict ambient rank drop on the boundary — original Lemma 6.7.**</p><p>Let \\(J\\subset\\widehat G_{\\mathbb C}\\) be the reductive stabilizer of a retained component, with Lie algebra \\(\\mathfrak j\\). If\n\\[\ne'\\in\\overline{J.e}\\setminus J.e,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\dim(\\widehat G.e')&lt;\\dim(\\widehat G.e).\n\\]\nConsequently the ambient nilpotent orbits and their compact spectral sets are distinct.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a \\(J\\)-stable complement\n\\[\n\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}=\\mathfrak j\\oplus W,\n\\]\nusing characteristic-zero reductivity. The rank of \\(\\operatorname{ad}(e)\\) on \\(\\mathfrak j\\) is the \\(J\\)-orbit dimension and strictly drops on a proper boundary orbit. Rank on \\(W\\) cannot increase under specialization, because a matrix rank bound is closed. Therefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rk}(\\operatorname{ad}(e')|_{\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}})\n&lt;\n\\operatorname{rk}(\\operatorname{ad}(e)|_{\\widehat{\\mathfrak g}}).\n\\]\nThese ranks are ambient orbit dimensions. For disconnected \\(J\\), orbits are finite unions of identity-component orbits, and the same argument applies. Proposition 9 gives disjoint compact sets.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Independence of the coefficient isomorphism — original Proposition 7.3.** **Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.** Use the setting of Theorem 1 and a fixed closed invariant \\(Y\\subset\\widehat{\\mathcal N}\\).", "statement": "<p>**Independence of the coefficient isomorphism — original Proposition 7.3.**</p><p>**Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.**\nUse the setting of Theorem 1 and a fixed closed invariant \\(Y\\subset\\widehat{\\mathcal N}\\). Regard algebraically closed characteristic-zero coefficient fields as abstract fields. Both \\(E=\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_\\ell\\) and \\(\\mathbb C\\) have transcendence degree \\(2^{\\aleph_0}\\) over \\(\\mathbb Q\\), so abstract isomorphisms \\(E\\simeq\\mathbb C\\) exist; no continuity or prescribed restriction to algebraic numbers is imposed.</p><p>**Statement.**\nAfter matching nilpotent orbits through the pinned split root datum, the subspace\n\\[\n\\iota_*(\\mathcal F_Y\\mathcal A_E)\\subset\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb C}\n\\]\nis independent of the isomorphism \\(\\iota:E\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\mathbb C\\).</p><p>Consequently \\(\\mathcal F_Y\\mathcal A_E\\) is invariant under every \\(\\tau\\in\\operatorname{Aut}(E/\\mathbb Q)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The point functions identify every transported ambient space with the same \\(\\mathbb C^{(S)}\\). The pinned split form identifies characters by highest weight. The pairing has rational coefficients, and spherical Hecke correspondences count the same finite-field modifications. Choose the positive square-root normalization on the complex side, giving the same \\(T_D\\) for every \\(\\iota\\).</p><p>If transported \\(\\sqrt q\\) differs by sign, set\n\\[\nz=(2\\rho_G)(-1)\\in Z(\\widehat G)[2],\n\\]\nwhere \\(2\\rho_G\\), the sum of positive roots of \\(G\\), is a cocharacter of the dual group. At a degree-\\(d\\) point, Satake parity introduces \\(z^d\\): highest weight \\(\\mu\\) acquires\n\\[\n(-1)^{d\\langle2\\rho_G,\\mu\\rangle},\n\\]\nits central character at \\(z^d\\), as encoded by the modified dual group [RS, Remark 6.7 and §6.4]. The measure changes by the corresponding central-translation pushforward. Proposition 9 says this preserves every \\(B_o\\). Contragredient conventions similarly preserve them by inversion.</p><p>Nilpotent orbits are matched through the pinned rational form and triple cocharacters, equivalently weighted Dynkin diagrams. No outer automorphism is introduced. Thus \\(Y\\)’s labels and \\(B_Y\\) are independent of \\(\\iota\\).</p><p>Propositions 9–11 characterize the transported subspace by identical moment equations and the same support test for every \\(g\\in\\mathcal A_{\\mathbb C}\\). The subspaces are equal.</p><p>For \\(\\tau\\in\\operatorname{Aut}(E/\\mathbb Q)\\), compare \\(\\iota\\) and \\(\\iota\\circ\\tau\\):\n\\[\n\\iota_*(\\mathcal F_Y\\mathcal A_E)\n=\n\\iota_*\\tau_*(\\mathcal F_Y\\mathcal A_E).\n\\]\nApply \\(\\iota^{-1}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Descent on finite coordinate sets — original Lemma 7.4.** **Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.** Here \\(F\\) is any algebraically closed extension of \\(\\mathbb Q\\), replacing the fixed coefficient field for this …", "statement": "<p>**Descent on finite coordinate sets — original Lemma 7.4.**</p><p>**Assumptions, conditions, and definitions.**\nHere \\(F\\) is any algebraically closed extension of \\(\\mathbb Q\\), replacing the fixed coefficient field for this general linear-algebra statement. Let \\(V_{\\mathbb Q}\\) have a specified, possibly infinite, basis. Let\n\\[\nW\\subset F\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}V_{\\mathbb Q}\n\\]\nbe invariant under every automorphism in \\(\\operatorname{Aut}(F/\\mathbb Q)\\), acting on coefficients in that basis.</p><p>**Statement.**\nThe natural map\n\\[\nF\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}(W\\cap V_{\\mathbb Q})\\longrightarrow W\n\\]\nis an isomorphism.</p>", "proof": "<p>The fixed field of \\(\\operatorname{Aut}(F/\\mathbb Q)\\) is \\(\\mathbb Q\\). An algebraic element outside \\(\\mathbb Q\\) has another conjugate, and the corresponding number-field embedding extends to an automorphism of \\(F\\). A transcendental element belongs to a transcendence basis; substitution \\(t\\mapsto t+1\\) extends from the rational function field to its algebraic closure \\(F\\), moving it.</p><p>For a finite ordered subset \\(I\\) of the specified basis, put\n\\[\nW_I=W\\cap F^I.\n\\]\nThis invariant finite-dimensional subspace has a unique reduced row-echelon basis matrix. Every automorphism preserves its row space, pivot positions, and normalization conditions, so leaves the matrix unchanged. Its entries lie in the fixed field \\(\\mathbb Q\\). Therefore\n\\[\nW_I=F\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}(W_I\\cap\\mathbb Q^I).\n\\]</p><p>Every vector has finite basis support. Directed union over \\(I\\) proves surjectivity. Tensoring\n\\[\nW\\cap V_{\\mathbb Q}\\hookrightarrow V_{\\mathbb Q}\n\\]\nwith the flat \\(\\mathbb Q\\)-module \\(F\\) proves injectivity.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [2, 3, "cite"], [2, 5, "cite"], [2, 7, "cite"], [2, 14, "cite"], [2, 19, "cite"], [3, 7, "cite"], [3, 8, "cite"], [3, 9, "cite"], [3, 14, "cite"], [3, 18, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [6, 3, "cite"], [7, 8, "cite"], [7, 11, "cite"], [7, 12, "cite"], [7, 13, "cite"], [7, 14, "cite"], [7, 18, "cite"], [7, 20, "cite"], [7, 22, "cite"], [8, 11, "cite"], [8, 12, "cite"], [8, 13, "cite"], [8, 14, "cite"], [8, 16, "cite"], [9, 8, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [10, 14, "cite"], [11, 14, "cite"], [11, 16, "cite"], [11, 22, "cite"], [12, 14, "cite"], [12, 16, "cite"], [12, 18, "cite"], [12, 22, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [13, 16, "cite"], [13, 22, "cite"], [14, 0, "cite"], [14, 18, "cite"], [14, 19, "cite"], [14, 21, "cite"], [15, 7, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 14, "cite"], [16, 20, "cite"], [17, 1, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [17, 21, "cite"], [17, 24, "cite"], [17, 25, "cite"], [18, 0, "cite"], [18, 1, "cite"], [18, 21, "cite"], [18, 25, "cite"], [19, 18, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [20, 18, "cite"], [21, 0, "cite"], [21, 1, "cite"], [21, 25, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [23, 21, "cite"], [24, 21, "cite"], [25, 0, "cite"], [26, 0, "cite"]]});
